    <Folder>
      <name>Hotels</name>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Peridot Grand Luxury Boutique</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-hotels-t5</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Hoan Kiem, Old Quarter<br/>5 stars<br/>$80-$137/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A 5-star boutique hotel a few minutes' walk from Hoan Kiem Lake, with a rooftop pool and bar looking over the Old Quarter roofline.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Best rating-to-location ratio of any hotel in the four agent proposals<br/><a href="https://www.booking.com/hotel/vn/peridot-grand-amp-spa-by-aira-hoan-kiem1.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://guide.michelin.com/en/hotels-stays/hanoi">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0330476,105.8462971&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0330476, 105.8462971]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8462971,21.0330476,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>GM Premium Hotel</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-hotels-t5</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Hoan Kiem, Old Quarter<br/>5 stars (self-declared)<br/>$58-$162/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A small Indochine-style hotel a short walk from Hoan Kiem Lake, with a rooftop pool and bar and a lobby styled like a private residence rather than a chain hotel.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Markets itself as 5-star and is priced and reviewed like one, but the rating is self-declared rather than issued by a tourism authority<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g293924-d24001394-Reviews-GM_Premium_Hotel-Hanoi.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.agoda.com/gm-premium-hotel_2/hotel/hanoi-vn.html">source 2</a> &middot; <a href="https://guide.michelin.com/en/hotels-stays/hoan-kiem/gm-premium-hotel-15158">source 3</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0307612,105.8481999&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0307612, 105.8481999]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8481999,21.0307612,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>La Sinfonia del Rey Hotel &amp; Spa</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-hotels-t4</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Hoan Kiem, opposite Hoan Kiem Lake<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/>4 stars (self-declared)<br/>$55-$130/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A neoclassical-styled hotel directly across from Hoan Kiem Lake, with a sky bar and a lake-view restaurant on the upper floors.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Unbeatable lake-front position; guest reviews and marketing describe it as 5-star but third-party listings place it at 4-star, so treat the rating as self-promoted<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g293924-d17627438-Reviews-La_Sinfonia_del_Rey_Hotel_Spa-Hanoi.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.booking.com/hotel/vn/la-sinfonia-del-rey-amp-spa.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0311556,105.8541135&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0311556, 105.8541135]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8541135,21.0311556,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Hanoi La Storia Hotel</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-hotels-t3</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Old Quarter, near Hang Ma Street and Dong Xuan Market<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/>3 stars<br/>$25-$50/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>An 18-room hotel on a quiet Old Quarter street four minutes' walk from Dong Xuan Market, with soundproofed rooms and an in-room computer in some categories.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Officially rated 3.0-star with a genuine budget price point and strong, if imperfect, reviews<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g293924-d12154091-Reviews-Hanoi_La_Storia_Hotel-Hanoi.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.traveloka.com/en-en/hotel/vietnam/hanoi-la-storia-hotel--4000000389115">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0357127,105.847721&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0357127, 105.847721]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.847721,21.0357127,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Pao's Sapa Leisure Hotel</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-sapa-hotels-t5</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Sapa</b> &middot; Sapa town hillside, Muong Hoa Valley view<br/>5 stars<br/>$90-$180/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A 223-room resort built into the hillside with terrace views over the Muong Hoa rice-terrace valley and Fansipan, run by CTX Holdings.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Ranked #3 of 180+ Sapa hotels on Tripadvisor and independently confirmed as officially 5-star; note that several reviewers say the room quality feels closer to a solid 3-star by Western standards, so treat the rating as location- and facility-driven rather than a guarantee of finish quality<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g311304-d12786956-Reviews-Pao_s_Sapa_Leisure_Hotel-Sapa_Lao_Cai_Province.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.trip.com/hotels/sapa-hotel-detail-10540093/pao-s-sapa-leisure-hotel/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=22.3270114,103.8466396&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 22.3270114, 103.8466396]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>103.8466396,22.3270114,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Ninh Binh Legend Hotel</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-ninh-binh-hotels-t5</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ninh Binh</b> &middot; City center<br/>5 stars<br/>$58-$120/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A 260-room hotel in the middle of Ninh Binh city with three swimming pools, an indoor pool, outdoor tennis courts, sauna and gym.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The city-center 5-star option, for a stay closer to restaurants and the train station rather than out among the karsts<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g303945-d1761735-Reviews-Ninh_Binh_Legend_Hotel-Ninh_Binh_Ninh_Binh_Province.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.kayak.com/Ninh-Binh-Hotels-Ninh-Binh-Legend-Hotel.376440.ksp">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=20.2744939,105.9587265&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 20.2744939, 105.9587265]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.9587265,20.2744939,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Phong Nha Farmstay</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-phong-nha-hotels-t3</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Phong Nha</b> &middot; Cu Nam, ~10km from Phong Nha town<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/>3 stars (self-declared)<br/>$14-$34/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>The original farmstay of the area — simple rooms and a pool set among rice fields and grazing buffalo, run by an Australian-Vietnamese family with a restaurant and tour desk on site.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> TripAdvisor's #1-ranked property in the national park (1,500+ reviews) and one of the two hotels the project's own agents agreed on.<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g4014591-d1930272-Reviews-Phong_Nha_Farmstay-Phong_Nha_Ke_Bang_National_Park_Quang_Binh_Province.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.booking.com/hotel/vn/phong-nha-farmstay.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=17.5818037,106.3087403&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 17.5818037, 106.3087403]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.3087403,17.5818037,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Meliá Vinpearl Hue</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hue-hotels-t5</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hue</b> &middot; City center, near the Perfume River and Truong Tien Bridge<br/>5 stars<br/>$54-$200/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A high-rise 5-star hotel in the middle of Hue with a full-service spa, indoor pool, gym and sauna, a 10-minute walk from the Perfume River and Truong Tien Bridge.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The master-spec's own pick for a restored Hue visit, and the highest-reviewed international-chain 5-star in the city (9.4/10 from over 3,000 reviews)<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g293926-d15113220-Reviews-Melia_Vinpearl_Hue-Hue_Thua_Thien_Hue_Province.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.melia.com/en/hotels/vietnam/hue-city/melia-vinpearl-hue">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=16.463231,107.5941414&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 16.463231, 107.5941414]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>107.5941414,16.463231,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Eldora Hotel</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hue-hotels-t4</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hue</b> &middot; City center<br/>4 stars<br/>$35-$90/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>An 81-room boutique hotel with bright, wood-floored rooms in a renaissance-style building in central Hue.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> One of the few hotels in Hue with an unambiguous official 4-star classification rather than a self-declared one, and consistently praised for cleanliness<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g293926-d6416461-Reviews-Eldora_Hotel-Hue_Thua_Thien_Hue_Province.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.kayak.com/Hue-Hotels-Eldora-Hotel.2076224.ksp">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=16.4648595,107.5945502&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 16.4648595, 107.5945502]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>107.5945502,16.4648595,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Jade Scene Hotel</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hue-hotels-t3</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hue</b> &middot; 30/42 Nguyen Cong Tru Street, near the pub street and Perfume River embankment<br/>3 stars<br/>$33-$100/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A new hotel a short walk from Hue's pub street and the river embankment, with a small rooftop pool overlooking the city and a spa.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> One of the highest-rated hotels of any tier in Hue (9.2/10 from over 3,000 reviews) and an officially assessed 3-star, not a self-rated boutique<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g293926-d25179951-Reviews-Jade_Scene_Hotel-Hue_Thua_Thien_Hue_Province.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.booking.com/hotel/vn/jade-scene.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=16.4695305,107.5968967&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 16.4695305, 107.5968967]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>107.5968967,16.4695305,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Thanh Lich Hue Hotel</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hue-hotels-t3</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hue</b> &middot; City center<br/>3 stars (self-declared)<br/>$25-$54/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A 40-room hotel with air-conditioned rooms, minibars and an indoor pool and sauna, with a rooftop breakfast serving both Asian and Western options.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> A dependable budget-friendly city-center option (9.0/10 from over 900 reviews), though its 'plus' star billing is self-declared<br/><a href="https://www.momondo.com/hotels/hue/Thanh-Lich-Hue-Hotel.mhd2207716.ksp">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.kayak.com/Hue-Hotels-Thanh-Lich-2-Hotel.2207716.ksp">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=16.4703117,107.5959428&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 16.4703117, 107.5959428]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>107.5959428,16.4703117,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Silkotel Hoi An</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-hotels-t4</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; Cam Pho Ward, close to Old Town<br/>4 stars<br/>$41-$91/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A mid-size hotel with an outdoor pool, spa, gym and restaurant a short walk from the Old Town's riverside streets.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The master-spec's 'best value' pick for Hoi An, rated 9.1/10 from 1,700 reviews at roughly two-thirds the price of the resort-style 5-stars<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g298082-d10259528-Reviews-Silkotel_Hoi_An-Hoi_An_Quang_Nam_Province.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.hotelscombined.com/Hotel/Silkotel_Hoi_An.htm">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=15.8794893,108.3223668&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 15.8794893, 108.3223668]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.3223668,15.8794893,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Lasenta Boutique Hotel Hoian</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-hotels-t4</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; Edge of town, among rice fields<br/>4 stars<br/>$52-$120/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A boutique hotel on the edge of Hoi An with an infinity pool and 4th-floor bar looking out over open rice paddies, plus a free shuttle to both Old Town and the beach.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Master-spec's alternative pick for the rice-field-view infinity pool at a lower price point than La Siesta<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g298082-d10149768-Reviews-Lasenta_Boutique_Hotel_Hoian-Hoi_An_Quang_Nam_Province.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.vietnamcoracle.com/lasenta-boutique-hotel-hoi-an/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=15.8819958,108.3403954&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 15.8819958, 108.3403954]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.3403954,15.8819958,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Hoian Central Hotel</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-hotels-t3</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; In the heart of Hoi An, steps from the riverside<br/>3 stars<br/>$30-$69/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A small hotel right in the town center with an outdoor pool and free bicycle rental, close enough to the Old Town's riverside streets to walk everywhere.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> One of the best-located officially-rated 3-star hotels in town, with ratings above many pricier properties<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g298082-d13737482-Reviews-Hoian_Central_Hotel-Hoi_An_Quang_Nam_Province.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.klook.com/hotels/detail/113919-hoian-central-hotel/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=15.8797503,108.319306&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 15.8797503, 108.319306]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.319306,15.8797503,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Little Town Villa</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-hotels-t3</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; Near Hoi An Market, 5 minutes' walk from the Japanese Covered Bridge<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/>3 stars<br/>$25-$130/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A small villa-style property near the town market with a spa, outdoor pool and restaurant, five minutes' walk from the Japanese Covered Bridge.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Rated 5/5 on Tripadvisor from hundreds of reviews despite its official 3-star classification - well above its price bracket<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g298082-d12192622-Reviews-Little_Town_Villas-Hoi_An_Quang_Nam_Province.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.booking.com/hotel/vn/little-town-villa.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=15.8826344,108.3244197&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 15.8826344, 108.3244197]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.3244197,15.8826344,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Dakruco Hotel</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-buon-ma-thuot-hotels-t3</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Buon Ma Thuot</b> &middot; City center<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/>3 stars (self-declared)<br/>$28-$38/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A long-running business hotel in central Buon Ma Thuot, run by the state-owned Dak Lak Rubber company, with an outdoor pool, restaurant and bar.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The cheapest hotel in the centre with a genuinely high review score rather than an inflated one<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g670918-d1772273-Reviews-Dakruco_Hotel-Buon_Ma_Thuot_Dak_Lak_Province.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.momondo.com/hotels/buon-ma-thuot/Dakruco-Hotel.mhd373818.ksp">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=12.6933774,108.0660931&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 12.6933774, 108.0660931]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.0660931,12.6933774,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Lak Tented Camp</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-lak-lake-hotels-t3</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Lak Lake</b> &middot; Yang Tao, Lien Son, reached only by boat<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/>3 stars (self-declared)<br/>$60-$160/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A small eco-lodge accessible only by boat crossing, with 15 safari-style tents, four lakeside bungalows and a wooden longhouse restaurant on the shore of Lak Lake.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The consensus pick of two independently-researched agent proposals for this leg, and the only proper lodge actually on the lake<br/><a href="https://www.booking.com/hotel/vn/lak-tented-camp.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g3389563-d11875783-Reviews-Lak_Tented_Camp-Lien_Son_Dak_Lak_Province.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=12.4279787,108.1812233&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 12.4279787, 108.1812233]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.1812233,12.4279787,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Bao Dai Villa (Lak Lake)</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-lak-lake-hotels-t3</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Lak Lake</b> &middot; Hill above Lak Lake, Lien Son town<br/>3 stars (self-declared)<br/>$20-$30/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>The 1951 hunting lodge Emperor Bao Dai built on a hill overlooking Lak Lake, now run as a simple guesthouse with the old dining room serving as a restaurant.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The only accommodation directly on the hill above the lake, inside a genuine former royal building<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g670918-d3291911-Reviews-Bao_Dai_Villa-Buon_Ma_Thuot_Dak_Lak_Province.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://vnexpress.net/biet-dien-bao-dai-ben-ho-lak-4393734.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=12.4155999,108.1816523&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 12.4155999, 108.1816523]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.1816523,12.4155999,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>MerPerle Dalat Hotel</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-da-lat-hotels-t5</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Da Lat</b> &middot; Next to Da Lat golf course<br/>5 stars (self-declared)<br/>$55-$91/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A large new hotel with an indoor pool, spa and its own attached winery, on the edge of downtown next to Da Lat's golf course.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The highest-scoring self-declared 5-star in Da Lat, though several guests say it falls short of a true 5-star standard<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g293922-d26305215-Reviews-MerPerle_Dalat_Hotel-Da_Lat_Lam_Dong_Province.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.kayak.com/Dalat-Hotels-Merperle-Dalat-Hotel.9967439.ksp">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=11.9410283,108.4583231&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 11.9410283, 108.4583231]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.4583231,11.9410283,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Colline Dalat</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-da-lat-hotels-t4</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Da Lat</b> &middot; Next to Da Lat night market<br/>4 stars<br/>$63-$76/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A 150-room modern hotel built directly onto the steps down to Da Lat's night market, with a rooftop restaurant looking over the town.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Officially 4-star and zero steps from the night market; the master-spec's top pick for Da Lat<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g293922-d16731336-Reviews-Colline-Da_Lat_Lam_Dong_Province.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://us.trip.com/hotels/dalat-hotel-detail-29515862/colline-dalat/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=11.94397,108.438126&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 11.94397, 108.438126]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.438126,11.94397,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Terracotta Hotel &amp; Resort Dalat</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-da-lat-hotels-t4</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Da Lat</b> &middot; Lakeside, near Da Lat golf course<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/>4 stars (self-declared)<br/>$44-$53/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A spa hotel on the shore of a small private lake, with two restaurants, bike rental and an adjoining golf course.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> A genuine lakeside 4-star property at close to 3-star prices<br/><a href="https://www.momondo.com/hotels/da-lat/Terracotta-Hotel-Resort-Dalat.mhd2272019.ksp">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.makemytrip.com/hotels-international/en-us/vietnam/dalat-hotels/terracotta_hotel_resort_dalat-details.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=11.894725,108.4397193&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 11.894725, 108.4397193]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.4397193,11.894725,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Hotel Des Arts Saigon - MGallery</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-saigon-hotels-t5</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ho Chi Minh City</b> &middot; District 1, near Notre-Dame Cathedral<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/>5 stars<br/>$134-$396/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><i>upper room types exceed $200</i><br/><br/>A 5-star Art Deco-era building restored as an Accor MGallery hotel, with 168 rooms, a rooftop infinity pool and a French colonial facade a short walk from Notre-Dame Cathedral.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Accor-chain 5-star with an officially assigned rating, mentioned as an alternative in the project's own vetted hotel research<br/><a href="https://www.hoteldesartssaigon.com/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g293925-d8142973-Reviews-Hotel_Des_Arts_Saigon_Mgallery-Ho_Chi_Minh_City.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.7820238,106.6971407&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.7820238, 106.6971407]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.6971407,10.7820238,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Silverland Central Hotel</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-saigon-hotels-t3</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ho Chi Minh City</b> &middot; District 1, near Ben Thanh Market<br/>3 stars<br/>$177-$188/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A 101-room officially-rated 3-star boutique hotel a short walk from Ben Thanh Market, part of the long-running Silverland hotel group, with a rooftop bar called OMG.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Officially classified 3-star (not self-declared) with a decade of top-ten 3-star rankings in the city<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g293925-d1178145-Reviews-Silverland_Central_Hotel-Ho_Chi_Minh_City.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://silverlandhotels.com/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.7718774,106.6972524&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.7718774, 106.6972524]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.6972524,10.7718774,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Bong Sen Hotel Saigon</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-saigon-hotels-t3</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ho Chi Minh City</b> &middot; District 1, Dong Khoi Street<br/>3 stars<br/>$48-$94/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A 120-room 3-star hotel on Dong Khoi, the city's main shopping street, with three restaurants and a small fitness center.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Officially rated 3-star on the same street as the 5-star picks, at a fraction of the price<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g293925-d1748426-Reviews-Bong_Sen_Hotel_Saigon-Ho_Chi_Minh_City.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.booking.com/hotel/vn/bong-sen-saigon.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.7751171,106.7038852&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.7751171, 106.7038852]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.7038852,10.7751171,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sheraton Can Tho</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-mekong-hotels-t5</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Mekong and Can Tho</b> &middot; Ninh Kieu, riverside<br/>5 stars<br/>$61-$200/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>The tallest international hotel in the Mekong Delta, on the Can Tho riverfront near Ninh Kieu Pier and the night market, formerly branded as Vinpearl Hotel Can Tho before its rebrand under Marriott.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The only internationally-chained 5-star in the Mekong Delta, confirmed by multiple independent sources as officially rated<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g303942-d11743875-Reviews-Sheraton_Can_Tho-Can_Tho_Mekong_Delta.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.marriott.com/en-us/hotels/vcasi-sheraton-can-tho/overview/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.0242294,105.7744816&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.0242294, 105.7744816]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.7744816,10.0242294,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Victoria Can Tho Resort</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-mekong-hotels-t4</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Mekong and Can Tho</b> &middot; Riverside, Cai Khe islet<br/>4 stars<br/>$71-$95/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A colonial-style riverside resort from the established Victoria hotels chain, with a pool overlooking the Can Tho River and its own daily Mekong boat tours for guests.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The delta's best-regarded conventional resort chain, officially rated and consistently praised over the larger conference hotels in town<br/><a href="https://www.travelfish.org/accommodation_profile/vietnam/mekong_delta/can_tho/can_tho/all/8497">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g303942-d306941-Reviews-Victoria_Can_Tho_Resort-Can_Tho_Mekong_Delta.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.0393054,105.7930514&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.0393054, 105.7930514]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.7930514,10.0393054,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Can Tho Ecolodge</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-mekong-hotels-t4</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Mekong and Can Tho</b> &middot; Cai Rang, Ba Lang River<br/>4 stars (self-declared)<br/>$56-$82/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A thatched-house eco-resort on the Ba Lang River outside town, with a pool, garden and bar, built around responsibly showcasing the surrounding delta rather than a conventional hotel tower.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The most resort-like eco-lodge option in the area, ranked third of Can Tho's specialty lodging with consistently strong reviews<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g303942-d17528812-Reviews-Can_Tho_Ecolodge-Can_Tho_Mekong_Delta.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.booking.com/hotel/vn/can-tho-ecolodge.en-gb.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=9.9878302,105.7414525&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 9.9878302, 105.7414525]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.7414525,9.9878302,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Bamboo Eco Village</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-mekong-hotels-t3</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Mekong and Can Tho</b> &middot; Can Tho outskirts<br/>3 stars<br/>$33-$56/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A small officially 3-star eco-hotel with a garden, pool and bicycles for guests, built around orchard and canal surroundings rather than a town-centre location.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Officially rated 3-star with unusually strong reviews for the tier, praised repeatedly for the garden and pool<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g303942-d19602577-Reviews-Bamboo_Eco_Village-Can_Tho_Mekong_Delta.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://en.planetofhotels.com/vietnam/can-tho/bamboo-eco-village">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.0074606,105.7054798&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.0074606, 105.7054798]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.7054798,10.0074606,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sheraton Phu Quoc Long Beach Resort</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-phu-quoc-hotels-t5</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Phu Quoc</b> &middot; Long Beach, Ganh Dau<br/>5 stars<br/>$97-$200/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A large Marriott-chain beachfront resort on Long Beach with multiple pools, several restaurants and direct sand access.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Officially rated 5-star with an international chain standard, and its entry-level rooms stay under the trip's price cap despite the brand<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g12666019-d8774806-Reviews-Sheraton_Phu_Quoc_Long_Beach_Resort-Ganh_Dau_Phu_Quoc_Island_Kien_Giang_Province.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.momondo.com/hotels/phu-quoc/Sheraton-Phu-Quoc-Long-Beach-Resort.mhd2449378.ksp">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.3375473,103.8498843&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.3375473, 103.8498843]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>103.8498843,10.3375473,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>L'Azure Resort and Spa</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-phu-quoc-hotels-t4</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Phu Quoc</b> &middot; Duong Dong<br/>4 stars<br/>$63-$115/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A small rustic-eco resort with a private sand beach, pool and spa, officially rated 4-star, built around a low-rise, garden-heavy design rather than a tower.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Officially rated 4-star with a 9.4-9.5 guest score, and the most consistently well-reviewed 4-star in the pool<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g1184679-d640629-Reviews-L_Azure_Resort_and_Spa-Duong_Dong_Phu_Quoc_Island_Kien_Giang_Province.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.makemytrip.com/hotels-international/vietnam/phu_quoc-hotels/l_azure_resort_and_spa-details.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.2080717,103.9616615&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.2080717, 103.9616615]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>103.9616615,10.2080717,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>AVS Hotel Phu Quoc</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-phu-quoc-hotels-t4</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Phu Quoc</b> &middot; Duong Dong, 100m from the beach<br/>4 stars<br/>$46-$156/night <i>(indicative, checked 2026-08-16)</i><br/><br/>A modern mid-size hotel a hundred metres from the beach, officially rated 4-star, with a pool, spa and airport transfers.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Officially rated 4-star, ranked in the top 20 hotels on the island by guest reviews despite its budget-adjacent price<br/><a href="https://en.planetofhotels.com/vietnam/phu-quoc/avs-hotel-phu-quoc">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g1184679-d20200026-Reviews-AVS_Hotel_Phu_Quoc-Duong_Dong_Phu_Quoc_Island_Kien_Giang_Province.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.2007569,103.9638791&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.2007569, 103.9638791]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>103.9638791,10.2007569,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
    </Folder>
    <Folder>
      <name>Must-see</name>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Hoan Kiem Lake</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Hoan Kiem<br/><br/>The lake at the centre of old Hanoi, with a small red bridge to a temple island. Locals walk and exercise around it at first light.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The city organises itself around this lake, and walking it is free<br/><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ho%C3%A0n_Ki%E1%BA%BFm_Lake">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hoan+Kiem+Lake+Hanoi+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0288313,105.8525357&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0288313, 105.8525357]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8525357,21.0288313,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Temple of Literature</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Dong Da, Van Mieu street<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A walled 2.5-hectare Confucian temple complex founded in 1070, with five courtyards, stone stelae on turtle bases recording centuries of exam graduates, and a wooden hall dedicated to Confucius.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Vietnam's first national university and one of Hanoi's oldest intact historical sites<br/><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Literature,_Hanoi">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Temple+of+Literature+Hanoi+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0287903,105.8359533&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0287903, 105.8359533]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8359533,21.0287903,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Hoa Lo Prison Museum</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Hoan Kiem, Hoa Lo street<br/><br/>A preserved wing of a French colonial-era prison built in 1896, nicknamed the "Hanoi Hilton" by American POWs held there in the 1960s-70s. Exhibits include a guillotine and the cell blocks used to hold Vietnamese independence activists.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> A blunt, unfiltered look at both the colonial and American-war periods of Vietnamese history, in the middle of the modern city<br/><a href="https://www.lonelyplanet.com/vietnam/hanoi/attractions/hoa-lo-prison-museum/a/poi-sig/1141862/357880">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hoa+Lo+Prison+Museum+Hanoi+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0254222,105.8465093&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0254222, 105.8465093]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8465093,21.0254222,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Imperial Citadel of Thang Long</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Ba Dinh, Hoang Dieu street<br/><br/>The excavated core of the royal citadel that was Vietnam's seat of power for about thirteen centuries, with the Doan Mon gate, the Kinh Thien palace foundations and an archaeological dig showing layers from the 7th century onward.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> A UNESCO World Heritage site ten minutes from the Old Quarter. Closed on Mondays, which rules out 26/10 and 23/11; the Friday and Saturday night tour does not fit either Hanoi stay<br/><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imperial_Citadel_of_Th%C4%83ng_Long">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://vinwonders.com/en/wonderpedia/news/imperial-citadel-of-thang-long/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Imperial+Citadel+of+Thang+Long+Hanoi+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0334391,105.83953&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0334391, 105.83953]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.83953,21.0334391,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Ho Chi Minh Mausoleum Complex</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Ba Dinh Square<br/><br/>The granite mausoleum holding Ho Chi Minh's embalmed body, facing Ba Dinh Square where independence was declared in 1945. The same complex holds his stilt house, the Presidential Palace grounds and the One Pillar Pagoda, a small wooden shrine on a single stone column in a lotus pond.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The mausoleum is closed for its annual maintenance from 4/9 to 2/11/2026, so the October stay misses it. It reopens 3/11, mornings only and closed Mondays and Fridays, which leaves the morning of 24/11 as the one window if the flight allows. Ba Dinh Square and the One Pillar Pagoda stay open regardless<br/><a href="https://en.sggp.org.vn/ho-chi-minh-mausoleum-to-close-for-restoration-from-september-4-post128741.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://en.vietnamplus.vn/president-ho-chi-minh-mausoleum-to-be-closed-for-regular-maintenance-post288232.vnp">source 2</a> &middot; <a href="https://hanoifreeprivatetourguide.com/one-pillar-pagoda-hanoi/">source 3</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ho+Chi+Minh+Mausoleum+Complex+Hanoi+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0367831,105.8346888&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0367831, 105.8346888]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8346888,21.0367831,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Ma Pi Leng Pass</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-ha-giang-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ha Giang</b> &middot; National Road 4C, between Dong Van and Meo Vac<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A 20km stretch of road called the "Happiness Road", carved into a sheer limestone cliff face at over 2,000m, hugging the mountainside with a straight drop of hundreds of metres to the turquoise Nho Que River below.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> One of Vietnam's "Four Great Passes" and the single most photographed stretch of the entire Ha Giang loop<br/><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M%C3%A3_P%C3%AD_L%C3%A8ng_Pass">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ma+Pi+Leng+Pass+Ha+Giang+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=23.2419571,105.3979217&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 23.2419571, 105.3979217]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.3979217,23.2419571,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Lung Cu Flag Tower</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-ha-giang-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ha Giang</b> &middot; Lung Cu commune, Dong Van district<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A 34.85m octagonal tower on a peak near Vietnam's northernmost point, flying a red flag with a yellow star that covers 54 square metres, one for each recognised ethnic group. Reached by roughly 280-800 stone steps depending on where you park.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The symbolic "top of Vietnam" and a standard end-goal of the loop's northern leg<br/><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lung_Cu_Flag_Tower">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lung+Cu+Flag+Tower+Ha+Giang+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=23.3634614,105.3163342&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 23.3634614, 105.3163342]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.3163342,23.3634614,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Fansipan cable car and Muong Hoa mountain train</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-sapa-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Sapa</b> &middot; Sun Plaza, Sapa town, to Fansipan summit<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A combined mountain train (Sun Plaza to Muong Hoa station) and a 6.3km cable car climbing to near the summit of Fansipan, at 3,143m the highest peak in Indochina, with a pagoda complex at the top station.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The easiest way to stand on the "Roof of Indochina" without a multi-day trek, and the combo ticket includes both legs<br/><a href="https://junglebosstours.com/explorer/tourism-blog/fansipan-mountain-sapa">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fansipan+cable+car+and+Muong+Hoa+mountain+train+Sapa+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=22.3218699,103.7995338&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 22.3218699, 103.7995338]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>103.7995338,22.3218699,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Cat Cat Village</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-sapa-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Sapa</b> &middot; San Sa Ho commune, about 3km from Sapa town<br/><br/>A Black H'mong village at the entrance to the Muong Hoa Valley, founded in the mid-19th century, with three streams meeting at Cat Cat waterfall and two chain footbridges crossing it.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The most accessible ethnic-minority village from Sapa town, walkable in an afternoon<br/><a href="https://vinpearl.com/en/cat-cat-village-sapa">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cat+Cat+Village+Sapa+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=22.3309642,103.8340516&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 22.3309642, 103.8340516]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>103.8340516,22.3309642,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Ha Long Bay limestone karsts</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-ha-long-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ha Long and Cat Ba</b> &middot; Ha Long Bay, Quang Ninh province<br/><br/>Roughly 1,600 limestone islands and karst pillars rising from emerald-green water, many topped with vegetation and containing caves; a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1994.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The reason this leg of the trip exists — best seen slowly, from the deck of an overnight boat<br/><a href="https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/672/">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ha+Long+Bay+limestone+karsts+Ha+Long+and+Cat+Ba+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=20.9084384,107.0682782&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 20.9084384, 107.0682782]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>107.0682782,20.9084384,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Cat Ba National Park</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-ha-long-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ha Long and Cat Ba</b> &middot; Central Cat Ba Island<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A national park covering most of Cat Ba Island's interior, protecting tropical limestone forest, wetlands and mangroves; part of the joint Ha Long Bay-Cat Ba Archipelago UNESCO World Heritage Site and a UNESCO Biosphere Reserve since 2004.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The forested, trekkable counterpart to the bay's boat-only islands, and home to the critically endangered Cat Ba langur<br/><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C%C3%A1t_B%C3%A0_National_Park">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cat+Ba+National+Park+Ha+Long+and+Cat+Ba+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=20.8063272,107.0383362&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 20.8063272, 107.0383362]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>107.0383362,20.8063272,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Trang An Scenic Landscape Complex</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-ninh-binh-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ninh Binh</b> &middot; Ninh Hai Commune, Hoa Lu District, ~7km from Ninh Binh city<br/><br/>A UNESCO World Heritage limestone karst landscape toured by rowed sampan along a 3-hour river route that threads through nine natural cave tunnels beneath the cliffs.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The UNESCO-listed core of the whole 'Ha Long Bay on land' comparison, and the reason Ninh Binh is on the itinerary at all<br/><a href="https://wherearethosemorgans.com/trang-an-boat-tour/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.getyourguide.com/trang-an-l36900/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Trang+An+Scenic+Landscape+Complex+Ninh+Binh+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=20.2537827,105.9005014&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 20.2537827, 105.9005014]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.9005014,20.2537827,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tam Coc boat ride</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-ninh-binh-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ninh Binh</b> &middot; Van Lam Village, Ninh Hai Commune<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A shorter 2-hour sampan ride, often rowed by foot, along the Ngo Dong River between rice paddies and through three limestone tunnels (Hang Ca, Hang Hai, Hang Ba) that give Tam Coc its name.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The classic, more compact counterpart to Trang An - green rice-paddy scenery in the dry season, flooded gold fields at harvest<br/><a href="https://happytovisit.com/ninh-binh-hoa-lu-mua-cave-and-trang-an-tour-and-boat-ride/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://junglebosstours.com/explorer/tourism-blog/trang-an-boat-tour">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tam+Coc+boat+ride+Ninh+Binh+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=20.2163426,105.937457&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 20.2163426, 105.937457]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.937457,20.2163426,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Paradise Cave</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-phong-nha-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Phong Nha</b> &middot; Phong Nha-Ke Bang National Park<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A dry cave reached by a steep staircase and jungle path, then a kilometre of wooden walkway through a cavern hung with stalactites lit to look like an underground cathedral.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Regularly named one of the most beautiful caves in Vietnam, and doable without a guide or booking ahead<br/><a href="https://www.vietnamtourism.com/en/phong-nha-ke-bang-national-park-tickets-complete-2026-price-guide-and-cave-selection-2">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://oxalisadventure.com/paradise-cave-in-phong-nha/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Paradise+Cave+Phong+Nha+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=17.5192008,106.223045&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 17.5192008, 106.223045]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.223045,17.5192008,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Khai Dinh Tomb</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hue-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hue</b> &middot; Chau Chu Village, ~10km from Hue city<br/><br/>The last and most ornate of the Nguyen royal tombs, its concrete exterior built in a Gothic-tinged European style but its interior sarcophagus chamber an explosion of colorful ceramic-mosaic murals, reached by 127 stone steps.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The most visually striking of the seven royal tombs and unlike any other Nguyen monument in Hue<br/><a href="https://vinwonders.com/en/wonderpedia/news/khai-dinh-tomb-things-to-know-about-the-last-imperial-tomb-built-in-hue/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.lonelyplanet.com/vietnam/central-vietnam/hue/attractions/tomb-of-khai-dinh/a/poi-sig/1158037/357875">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Khai+Dinh+Tomb+Hue+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=16.3989537,107.5902801&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 16.3989537, 107.5902801]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>107.5902801,16.3989537,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Hoi An Ancient Town and Japanese Covered Bridge</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; Old Town, along the Thu Bon River<br/><br/>A UNESCO-listed trading-port town of yellow-walled shophouses, Chinese assembly halls and merchant homes dating to the 16th-18th centuries, centred on the 18-metre wooden Japanese Covered Bridge that doubles as a small temple.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The reason Hoi An exists on any itinerary - one of Southeast Asia's best-preserved trading ports, UNESCO-listed since 1999<br/><a href="https://vinpearl.com/en/japanese-bridge-hoi-an-a-cultural-symbol-of-the-ancient-town">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://grooviet.com/destination/japanese-covered-bridge/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hoi+An+Ancient+Town+and+Japanese+Covered+Bridge+Hoi+An+and+Da+Nang+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=15.8779509,108.3239898&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 15.8779509, 108.3239898]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.3239898,15.8779509,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Ba Na Hills and Golden Bridge</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; Ba Na Mountain, ~45 minutes west of Da Nang<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A hilltop resort reached by a record-length cable car, built around a recreated French hill-station village and the Golden Bridge - a walkway held up by two giant stone hands emerging from the hillside.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The consensus pick over My Son among the trip's competing proposals (route decision R6), and Central Vietnam's most photographed modern landmark<br/><a href="https://hoiandaytrip.com/ba-na-hills-tickets/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://happytovisit.com/golden-bridge-ba-na-hills-marble-mountain-monkey-mountain/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ba+Na+Hills+and+Golden+Bridge+Hoi+An+and+Da+Nang+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=16.0258471,108.0362621&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 16.0258471, 108.0362621]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.0362621,16.0258471,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>The World Coffee Museum</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-buon-ma-thuot-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Buon Ma Thuot</b> &middot; Tan Loi Ward, inside Trung Nguyen Coffee Village, ~4km from center<br/><br/>A museum built in the style of Central Highlands longhouses, holding over 10,000 coffee-related artefacts from around the world, with hands-on tasting rooms rather than glass display cases.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The signature sight of Vietnam's coffee capital, and the reason most visitors come to Buon Ma Thuot at all<br/><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_World_Coffee_Museum">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://vietnamtourism.gov.vn/en/post/20640">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The+World+Coffee+Museum+Buon+Ma+Thuot+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=12.6908584,108.0445203&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 12.6908584, 108.0445203]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.0445203,12.6908584,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Ako Dhong Village</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-buon-ma-thuot-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Buon Ma Thuot</b> &middot; Tan Loi Ward, inside the city<br/><br/>A working Ede ethnic-minority neighbourhood inside the city itself, with more than 30 traditional wooden longhouses built on stilts in the shape of boats.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The only place to see genuine Ede stilt-house architecture without leaving town<br/><a href="https://en.nhandan.vn/ako-dhong-village-a-door-to-explore-ede-ethnic-culture-in-the-heart-of-buon-ma-thuot-city-post146489.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://sayhellovietnam.com/ako-dhong-village-an-attractive-village-in-the-city-in-buon-ma-thuot/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ako+Dhong+Village+Buon+Ma+Thuot+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=12.696465,108.0491334&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 12.696465, 108.0491334]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.0491334,12.696465,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Dray Nur Waterfall</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-buon-ma-thuot-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Buon Ma Thuot</b> &middot; Krong Ana district, ~27km southwest of center<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A 250-metre-wide waterfall dropping more than 30 metres where the Krong Ana and Krong No rivers meet to form the Serepok River, crossed by a suspension bridge over the gorge.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The most powerful waterfall in the Central Highlands, and a straightforward half-day trip from the city<br/><a href="https://vinpearl.com/en/dray-nur-waterfall-dak-lak-things-to-do-here">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Attraction_Review-g670918-d4766576-Reviews-Dray_Nur_Waterfall-Buon_Ma_Thuot_Dak_Lak_Province.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dray+Nur+Waterfall+Buon+Ma+Thuot+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=12.5405291,107.8904044&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 12.5405291, 107.8904044]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>107.8904044,12.5405291,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Lak Lake</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-lak-lake-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Lak Lake</b> &middot; Lien Son, Lak district<br/><br/>The largest natural freshwater lake in the Central Highlands, ringed by rice paddies and forest, crossed by hand-carved dugout canoes rather than motorboats.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The centrepiece of this leg of the route, and the reason the itinerary stops here overnight<br/><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lak_Lake">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.bestpricetravel.com/travel-guide/lak-lake.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lak+Lake+Lak+Lake+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=12.4232112,108.1776243&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 12.4232112, 108.1776243]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.1776243,12.4232112,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Crazy House (Hang Nga Guesthouse)</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-da-lat-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Da Lat</b> &middot; Huynh Thuc Khang Street<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A surreal building shaped like a giant banyan tree, designed by architect Dang Viet Nga with tunnel staircases, mushroom-shaped rooms and almost no straight lines anywhere.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Unlike anything else built in Vietnam, and walkable from the city centre<br/><a href="https://www.cnn.com/travel/article/crazy-house-dalat-vietnam">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://vinwonders.com/en/wonderpedia/news/dalat-crazy-house/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Crazy+House+%28Hang+Nga+Guesthouse%29+Da+Lat+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=11.9345622,108.4305116&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 11.9345622, 108.4305116]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.4305116,11.9345622,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Langbiang Mountain</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-da-lat-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Da Lat</b> &middot; Lac Duong, ~12km north of the city<br/><br/>A 2,167m twin-peaked mountain reached by a bumpy 4.5km jeep track from its base, with panoramic views over Da Lat's pine forests and valleys from the summit.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The best viewpoint over the whole Da Lat plateau, and an easy half-day trip<br/><a href="https://vinwonders.com/en/wonderpedia/news/lang-biang-mountain-dalat/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://localvietnam.com/blog/langbiang-mountain/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Langbiang+Mountain+Da+Lat+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=12.0472573,108.4405826&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 12.0472573, 108.4405826]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.4405826,12.0472573,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Xuan Huong Lake</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-da-lat-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Da Lat</b> &middot; City center<br/><br/>A 40-hectare artificial lake built by French colonists in 1919 at the centre of Da Lat, ringed by a walking path, pine trees and the old golf course.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Da Lat organises itself around this lake the way Hanoi organises itself around Hoan Kiem<br/><a href="https://vinwonders.com/en/wonderpedia/news/xuan-huong-lake-dalat/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Attraction_Review-g293922-d451022-Reviews-Xuan_Huong_Lake-Da_Lat_Lam_Dong_Province.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Xuan+Huong+Lake+Da+Lat+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=11.9450803,108.4487222&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 11.9450803, 108.4487222]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.4487222,11.9450803,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Reunification Palace</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-saigon-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ho Chi Minh City</b> &middot; District 1<br/><br/>A 1960s modernist government building where a North Vietnamese tank crashed through the gates on 30 April 1975. The war rooms, radio room and presidential quarters are preserved as they were that day.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The single physical location where the Vietnam War's ending is preserved in place, not just described<br/><a href="https://www.getyourguide.com/explorer/ho-chi-minh-ttd272/landmarks-in-ho-chi-minh-city/">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Reunification+Palace+Ho+Chi+Minh+City+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.7770348,106.695488&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.7770348, 106.695488]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.695488,10.7770348,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Cai Rang Floating Market</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-mekong-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Mekong and Can Tho</b> &middot; Cai Rang, about 6 km southwest of central Can Tho<br/><br/>A river market where trading boats hang a sample of their goods from a tall pole so buyers spot it from the water. It is shrinking fast - around 400 boats at its 1990s peak, roughly 200 registered today, and reports of as few as 40 actually trading on a good morning as roads replaced the river. Tour boats now outnumber traders. It runs from before sunrise and is over as a wholesale market by mid-morning.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Still the largest floating market left in the delta, but go for what it is now rather than the photographs - and only at dawn. Touts and price gouging were reported through 2026. Phong Dien, upstream, sits on a channel too narrow for the big tour boats and keeps more real trading<br/><a href="https://vemekong.com/guide-to-can-tho-floating-markets/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.aljazeera.com/features/2025/2/28/vietnams-floating-markets-face-uncertain-future-as-life-moves-onshore">source 2</a> &middot; <a href="https://news.tuoitre.vn/are-business-and-management-practices-sinking-cai-rang-floating-market-in-vietnam-103260224205542246.htm">source 3</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cai+Rang+Floating+Market+Mekong+and+Can+Tho+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.0023957,105.7443925&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.0023957, 105.7443925]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.7443925,10.0023957,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Hon Thom Cable Car</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-phu-quoc-must_see</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Phu Quoc</b> &middot; An Thoi, southern Phu Quoc<br/><br/>A three-cable gondola line running almost 8 km from the An Thoi coast over open sea to Hon Thom island, in 30-passenger cabins over a 15-minute ride, passing above two smaller islands en route.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Guinness World Record holder for the longest three-wire sea-crossing cable car, and the way most visitors reach Hon Thom's Sun World park<br/><a href="https://blog.premierresidencesphuquoc.com/hon-thom-cable-car/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.vietjetair.com/en/news/travel-guides-1665635013747/hon-thom-phu-quoc-cable-car-review-the-worlds-longest-sea-crossing-cable-car-1768878737803">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hon+Thom+Cable+Car+Phu+Quoc+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=9.9920077,104.0124425&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 9.9920077, 104.0124425]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>104.0124425,9.9920077,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
    </Folder>
    <Folder>
      <name>Attractions &amp; Experiences</name>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Vietnam Museum of Ethnology</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Cau Giay, about 8km from the Old Quarter<br/><br/>An open-air and indoor museum on the 54 officially recognised ethnic groups of Vietnam, with full-size reconstructed stilt houses and tombs in the garden and 15,000+ artifacts inside.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The single best introduction to the hill-tribe cultures the trip later passes through in Sapa and Ha Giang<br/><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vietnam_Museum_of_Ethnology">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Vietnam+Museum+of+Ethnology+Hanoi+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0400774,105.7988403&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0400774, 105.7988403]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.7988403,21.0400774,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Thang Long Water Puppet Theatre</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Hoan Kiem, Dinh Tien Hoang street, north shore of the lake<br/><br/>A 50-minute show of wooden puppets performed on a pool of water by puppeteers hidden behind a bamboo screen, with a live band playing traditional instruments. The form comes from the flooded rice fields of the Red River delta.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> A northern Vietnamese art that exists nowhere else, indoors and an hour long, which makes it the natural plan for a rainy evening. Evening slots sell out two to three days ahead<br/><a href="https://vinwonders.com/en/wonderpedia/news/thang-long-water-puppet-theater-hanoi-enjoy-excellent-folk-art-of-vietnam/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://viet-go.com/en/attractions/thang-long-water-puppet-theatre-guide">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Thang+Long+Water+Puppet+Theatre+Hanoi+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0317043,105.853455&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0317043, 105.853455]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.853455,21.0317043,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Vietnamese Women's Museum</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Hoan Kiem, 36 Ly Thuong Kiet<br/><br/>Four floors on the role of women in Vietnamese family life, work, war and fashion, from ethnic-minority wedding dress to the street vendors with shoulder poles, with English labels throughout.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Consistently rated one of the best-curated museums in the city, cheap, indoors and a short walk from Hoan Kiem Lake<br/><a href="https://vietnamdiscovery.com/hanoi/attractions/vietnamese-womens-museum/">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Vietnamese+Women%27s+Museum+Hanoi+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0233506,105.8515958&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0233506, 105.8515958]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8515958,21.0233506,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>St. Joseph's Cathedral</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Hoan Kiem, Nha Chung street<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A neo-Gothic cathedral completed in 1886, modelled on Notre-Dame de Paris, its grey twin towers standing over a small square lined with cafes.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Free, five minutes from the lake, and the square in front is where Hanoians sit on low stools for lemon tea in the evening<br/><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Joseph%27s_Cathedral,_Hanoi">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=St.+Joseph%27s+Cathedral+Hanoi+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0286483,105.8488566&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0286483, 105.8488566]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8488566,21.0286483,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Long Bien Bridge</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Between the Old Quarter and Long Bien, over the Red River<br/><br/>A 1.7km steel cantilever bridge finished by the French in 1903, bombed repeatedly in the American war and patched rather than replaced. Trains run down the middle and the outer lanes carry only motorbikes, bicycles and pedestrians.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Free, and a walk out to the middle at sunset gives the widest view of the Red River and the city. The Ceramic Mosaic Mural starts at its foot<br/><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Long_Bi%C3%AAn_Bridge">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Long+Bien+Bridge+Hanoi+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0430652,105.8580334&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0430652, 105.8580334]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8580334,21.0430652,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Phung Hung Mural Street</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Old Quarter, Phung Hung street<br/><br/>The brick arches under the old railway viaduct on Phung Hung street, painted in 2017 by Vietnamese and Korean artists with scenes of old Hanoi - the lantern stalls of Hang Ma at Mid-Autumn, a street vendor, the Trang Tien department store.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> A free open-air gallery on the same street as the train-street cafes, so the two fit in one visit<br/><a href="https://vietnamnet.vn/en/phung-hung-mural-street-features-old-hanoi-E199459.html">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Phung+Hung+Mural+Street+Hanoi+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0340272,105.8457157&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0340272, 105.8457157]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8457157,21.0340272,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Bat Trang Pottery Village</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Gia Lam, about 13km south-east of the Old Quarter<br/><br/>A village that has made ceramics for over five hundred years, with a covered pottery market, family kilns and walk-in workshops where you throw your own piece on a wheel.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> A half-day trip within the city limits, free to enter. Bus 47A from Long Bien takes 30-40 minutes; a wheel session costs about 20,000 VND and firing a piece to take home a little more<br/><a href="https://vinpearl.com/en/bat-trang-pottery-village-experience-exciting-hands-on-pottery-making">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bat+Trang+Pottery+Village+Hanoi+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=20.9796328,105.9146403&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 20.9796328, 105.9146403]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.9146403,20.9796328,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Ha Giang Loop (self-drive or easy-rider motorbike route)</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-ha-giang-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ha Giang</b> &middot; Circuit from Ha Giang City through Quan Ba, Yen Minh, Dong Van, Meo Vac<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A roughly 350-400km loop of mountain roads through the Dong Van Karst Plateau Geopark, ridden over 3-4 days either as the driver (own or rented manual motorbike) or as a passenger behind a local "easy rider" guide.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The reason this region is on the route at all — hairpin passes, terraced valleys and H'mong villages are only reachable this way<br/><a href="https://vietnammotorcycletours.com/ha-giang-loop-vietnams-ultimate-motorbike-adventure">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ha+Giang+Loop+%28self-drive+or+easy-rider+motorbike+route%29+Ha+Giang+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=22.8372564,105.01257&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 22.8372564, 105.01257]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.01257,22.8372564,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Du Gia village and waterfall</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-ha-giang-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ha Giang</b> &middot; Du Gia commune, about 70km from Ha Giang City<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A Tay ethnic village of wooden stilt houses in a rice-field valley, with a swimmable turquoise-pooled waterfall a short walk away; usually reached only by motorbike or car since there is no direct bus.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The loop's quiet counterpoint to Dong Van/Meo Vac — a slower, greener detour with a place to swim<br/><a href="https://www.vietnamcoracle.com/to-day-du-gia-village-independent-review/">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Du+Gia+village+and+waterfall+Ha+Giang+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=22.9329158,105.2233287&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 22.9329158, 105.2233287]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.2233287,22.9329158,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Trung Trang Cave</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-ha-long-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ha Long and Cat Ba</b> &middot; Cat Ba National Park, about 20-30 minutes from the port<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A 300-metre natural cave carved through a mountain in the middle of Cat Ba's largest valley, with stalactites and stalagmites reachable on a short easy walk.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> A quick, low-effort cave stop that fits into a cruise itinerary alongside kayaking, without needing a dedicated caving day<br/><a href="https://www.bestpricetravel.com/travel-guide/trung-trang-cave.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.halongbaycruises.com/travel-guide/explore-cat-ba-island-with-halong-bay-cruises-trung-trang-cave.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Trung+Trang+Cave+Ha+Long+and+Cat+Ba+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=20.7887703,106.9974369&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 20.7887703, 106.9974369]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.9974369,20.7887703,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Hoa Lu Ancient Capital</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-ninh-binh-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ninh Binh</b> &middot; Truong Yen Commune, ~10km from Ninh Binh city<br/><br/>The restored temples of Dinh Tien Hoang and Le Dai Hanh, marking the site of Vietnam's 10th-century capital, set inside a ring of limestone mountains.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The historical anchor of the region, and usually combined with Trang An or Mua Cave into one day's route<br/><a href="https://happytovisit.com/ninh-binh-hoa-lu-bai-dinh-trang-an-and-mua-cave-tour/">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hoa+Lu+Ancient+Capital+Ninh+Binh+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=20.283483,105.8995986&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 20.283483, 105.8995986]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8995986,20.283483,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Bai Dinh Pagoda</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-ninh-binh-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ninh Binh</b> &middot; Gia Sinh Commune, ~15km from Ninh Binh city<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>The largest Buddhist temple complex in Vietnam, spread across a hillside with a giant bronze Buddha, hundreds of Arhat statues along covered corridors, and a bell tower housing one of Asia's largest bronze bells.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> A different register from the boat-and-karst circuit - scale and religious architecture rather than nature<br/><a href="https://happytovisit.com/ninh-binh-hoa-lu-bai-dinh-trang-an-and-mua-cave-tour/">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bai+Dinh+Pagoda+Ninh+Binh+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=20.2752292,105.8654697&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 20.2752292, 105.8654697]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8654697,20.2752292,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Dark Cave zipline and mud bath</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-phong-nha-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Phong Nha</b> &middot; Chay River, Phong Nha-Ke Bang National Park<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A short zipline across the Chay River into a pitch-black cave, followed by a swim to a natural mud pool deep inside, then kayaking back down the river.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The easiest adrenaline activity in Phong Nha — half a day, no fitness test, and the one every family and backpacker group does<br/><a href="https://www.getyourguide.com/phong-nha-l176859/phong-nha-cave-exploration-and-zipline-dark-cave-tour-t511831/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://junglebosstours.com/explorer/tourism-blog/dark-cave-phong-nha">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dark+Cave+zipline+and+mud+bath+Phong+Nha+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=17.5742556,106.2527864&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 17.5742556, 106.2527864]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.2527864,17.5742556,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>The Duck Stop</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-phong-nha-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Phong Nha</b> &middot; Bong Lai Valley, ~10km from Phong Nha town<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A small working duck farm where visitors feed and walk a flock of ducks along the paddy dikes, with a buffalo ride and a drink included.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> TripAdvisor's #1-ranked Phong Nha attraction and the easiest, lightest thing to do on a rest day between caves<br/><a href="https://theduckstop.vn/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://culturephamtravel.com/the-duck-stop/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The+Duck+Stop+Phong+Nha+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=17.6041976,106.3657674&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 17.6041976, 106.3657674]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.3657674,17.6041976,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Pub with Cold Beer</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-phong-nha-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Phong Nha</b> &middot; Bong Lai Valley, near the Rao Con River<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A rustic family farm-bar down a country lane where you pick a live chicken from the garden and it's grilled to order, eaten in hammocks by the river.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The best-known stop on the Bong Lai Valley motorbike loop and a genuinely local, unstaged meal<br/><a href="https://culturephamtravel.com/the-duck-stop/">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pub+with+Cold+Beer+Phong+Nha+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=17.5982371,106.3635278&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 17.5982371, 106.3635278]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.3635278,17.5982371,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tu Duc Tomb</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hue-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hue</b> &middot; Thuy Xuan Village, ~7km from Hue city<br/><br/>A landscaped royal retreat built around a lake in a pine-forested valley, where Emperor Tu Duc lived, wrote poetry and boated for years before his death, dotted with pavilions and temples rather than a single mausoleum building.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The gentlest and most park-like of the tombs - built as a living retreat, not just a burial site - and a contrast to Khai Dinh's grandeur<br/><a href="https://centralvietnamguide.com/tu-duc-tomb/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://vinpearl.com/en/tu-duc-tomb-one-of-the-most-appealing-attractions-in-hue-to-visit">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tu+Duc+Tomb+Hue+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=16.433089,107.5647017&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 16.433089, 107.5647017]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>107.5647017,16.433089,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Marble Mountains (Ngu Hanh Son)</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; Between Da Nang and Hoi An, on the coast road<br/><br/>Five limestone-and-marble hills, each named for one of the five elements, honeycombed with caves and grottoes containing Buddhist shrines; an elevator and stairs lead up to viewpoints and the Huyen Khong cave, once used as a wartime field hospital.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> A genuinely different landscape from Hoi An's flat riverside town, and an easy stop on the way to or from Da Nang<br/><a href="https://happytovisit.com/golden-bridge-ba-na-hills-marble-mountain-monkey-mountain/">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Marble+Mountains+%28Ngu+Hanh+Son%29+Hoi+An+and+Da+Nang+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=16.00402,108.2627745&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 16.00402, 108.2627745]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.2627745,16.00402,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>An Bang Beach surf lesson</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; An Bang Beach, ~3km east of Old Town<br/><br/>A stretch of sandy beach with gentle, beginner-friendly waves, where expat-run surf schools rent boards and run lessons; the surf season runs roughly September to March.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Hoi An's real board-sport option, and a 20-minute bike ride from the Old Town through rice paddies<br/><a href="https://thesurfatlas.com/vietnam-surf/hoi-an-surf/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://thedeckhousevietnam.com/water-sports/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=An+Bang+Beach+surf+lesson+Hoi+An+and+Da+Nang+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=15.9141779,108.3396551&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 15.9141779, 108.3396551]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.3396551,15.9141779,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Datanla Waterfall Canyoning</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-da-lat-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Da Lat</b> &middot; 7km south of the city<br/><br/>A guided descent through the Datanla waterfall gorge from a base camp at the falls: rappelling down cliffs and a 30m waterfall, a zipline, a water slide and a narrow rock chute nicknamed the 'washing machine'.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Da Lat's real adventure-sport claim, and one of the few canyoning operations anywhere in Vietnam<br/><a href="https://tinggly.com/experience/canyoning-2400m-alpine-coaster-activity-in-dalat/141620P6">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/AttractionProductReview-g293922-d27116487-Canyoning_2400m_Alpine_Coaster_Activity_in_Dalat-Da_Lat_Lam_Dong_Province.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Datanla+Waterfall+Canyoning+Da+Lat+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=11.9011774,108.4488444&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 11.9011774, 108.4488444]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.4488444,11.9011774,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tuyen Lam Lake SUP/Kayak and Clay Tunnel</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-da-lat-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Da Lat</b> &middot; Tuyen Lam Lake, south of the city<br/><br/>A paddle by kayak or stand-up paddleboard across Tuyen Lam Lake's pine-fringed water, usually combined with a stop at the Clay Tunnel, a walk-through open-air clay sculpture near the Truc Lam monastery.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The quiet, uncrowded counterpart to the night market; nature and folk art in one outing<br/><a href="https://powertraveller.com/da-lat-tuyen-lam-lake-kayak-or-sup-tour/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://kayawanderlust.com/self-guided-tour-to-the-clay-tunnel-and-truc-lam-monastery/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tuyen+Lam+Lake+SUP%2FKayak+and+Clay+Tunnel+Da+Lat+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=11.8907954,108.4250836&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 11.8907954, 108.4250836]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.4250836,11.8907954,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Cu Chi Tunnels</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-saigon-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ho Chi Minh City</b> &middot; Cu Chi District, about 1.5 hours from central Saigon<br/><br/>A preserved section of the more than 120 km of hand-dug underground tunnel network used by Viet Cong fighters, with widened crawl-through sections, hidden trapdoor entrances and reconstructed underground rooms.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The signature half-day trip from Saigon, and one of the few places in Vietnam you physically enter the war rather than just read about it<br/><a href="https://www.holidify.com/places/ho-chi-minh-city/sightseeing-and-things-to-do.html">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cu+Chi+Tunnels+Ho+Chi+Minh+City+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=11.0630436,106.5291244&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 11.0630436, 106.5291244]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.5291244,11.0630436,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>War Remnants Museum</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-saigon-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ho Chi Minh City</b> &middot; District 3<br/><br/>A three-storey museum housing over 20,000 artifacts, photographs and military hardware across nine permanent exhibitions on the Vietnam War and its aftermath, including an outdoor yard of captured US tanks and aircraft.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The most direct, unfiltered account of the war from the Vietnamese side, consistently rated the city's top museum<br/><a href="https://ahoyvietnam.com/war-remnants-museum/">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=War+Remnants+Museum+Ho+Chi+Minh+City+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.7793647,106.6922806&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.7793647, 106.6922806]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.6922806,10.7793647,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>My Tho and Ben Tre coconut candy village</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-mekong-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Mekong and Can Tho</b> &middot; My Tho and Ben Tre provinces, upper Mekong Delta<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A river-island circuit reached by motorboat and narrow-canal sampan (a small wooden boat rowed by a boatman in a conical hat), stopping at a small workshop where coconut milk is hand-cooked and cut into candy while still warm.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The classic, gentler alternative to Can Tho for a single-day Mekong visit, and the standard way to see hand-made coconut candy production<br/><a href="https://viet-go.com/en/attractions/my-tho-ben-tre-mekong-delta-guide">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=My+Tho+and+Ben+Tre+coconut+candy+village+Mekong+and+Can+Tho+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.0163692,105.7379094&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.0163692, 105.7379094]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.7379094,10.0163692,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Vinh Trang Pagoda</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-mekong-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Mekong and Can Tho</b> &middot; My Tho<br/><br/>A 19th-century Buddhist pagoda recognised as a national historical relic, blending Vietnamese, Khmer and French architectural styles with a large reclining Buddha statue in its grounds.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The most architecturally distinct temple in the delta, mixing three building traditions in one compound<br/><a href="https://www.viator.com/Mekong-Delta-attractions/Vinh-Trang-Pagoda/d23962-a19069">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Vinh+Trang+Pagoda+Mekong+and+Can+Tho+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.3628375,106.3735689&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.3628375, 106.3735689]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.3735689,10.3628375,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Vinpearl Safari Phu Quoc</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-phu-quoc-attractions</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Phu Quoc</b> &middot; Bai Dai, northwest Phu Quoc<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>Vietnam's largest wildlife conservation park, home to roughly 4,500 animals across 200 species including Bengal tigers, African lions, giraffes and white rhinos, viewable from a safari-style drive-through zone and walking areas.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The largest wildlife park in the country, and a full-day option that doesn't require a beach or a boat<br/><a href="https://vinwonders.com/en/wonderpedia/news/review-of-vinpearl-safari-phu-quoc/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Attraction_Review-g469418-d9748314-Reviews-Vinpearl_Safari_Phu_Quoc-Phu_Quoc_Island_Kien_Giang_Province.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Vinpearl+Safari+Phu+Quoc+Phu+Quoc+Vietnam" style="color:#cc0000;font-weight:700">Search YouTube</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.3393964,103.8950113&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.3393964, 103.8950113]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>103.8950113,10.3393964,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
    </Folder>
    <Folder>
      <name>Food</name>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Bun Cha Huong Lien</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-street_food</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Hai Ba Trung<br/><br/>A plain three-storey shop that has grilled pork patties over charcoal since the 1990s, served in a bowl of broth with noodles and herbs. Obama and Anthony Bourdain ate here in 2016 and the shop has traded on it since - prices rose, the plastic stools became chairs, and the queue is now mostly visitors.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The Obama shop, and worth it only if that is the point - it sits in the Michelin Guide selection but carries no Bib Gourmand, and locals rate the sauce and the marinade below what it was. Better bun cha is two streets away<br/><a href="https://guide.michelin.com/vn/en/ha-noi-municipality/hanoi/restaurant/bun-cha-huong-lien">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://deeptravelmag.com/is-the-obama-bun-cha-huong-lien-in-hanoi-worth-it-a-local-tells-all/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0180504,105.8538843&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0180504, 105.8538843]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8538843,21.0180504,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Pho Gia Truyen (Bat Dan)</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-street_food</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; 49 Bat Dan Street, Cua Dong Ward, Hoan Kiem<br/><br/>A bare-bones pho shop where you queue at the counter, pay first and carry your own bowl to a shared table. Beef only, in three cuts - flank, round steak and brisket.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The Old Quarter's benchmark beef pho and a Bib Gourmand; the queue at 07:00 is the review<br/><a href="https://guide.michelin.com/en/vn/ha-noi/ha-noi_2974158/restaurants/bib-gourmand">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://e.vnexpress.net/photo/food-recipes/hanois-best-pho-michelin-guide-picks-4931684.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0336377,105.8463792&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0336377, 105.8463792]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8463792,21.0336377,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Pho Ga Nguyet</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-street_food</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; 5B Phu Doan Street, Hang Trong Ward, Hoan Kiem<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A chicken-pho counter that lets you pick the cut - thigh, breast, or the whole lot with egg - in a clear broth. Open late, past midnight.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The one pho address on this list that is still serving at 23:00, which matters on an arrival night<br/><a href="https://guide.michelin.com/vn/en/ha-noi/ha-noi_2974158/restaurant/pho-ga-nguyet">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://guide.michelin.com/en/vn/ha-noi/ha-noi_2974158/restaurants/bib-gourmand">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0302913,105.8476483&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0302913, 105.8476483]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8476483,21.0302913,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Banh Cuon Gia Truyen Thanh Van (Hang Ga)</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-street_food</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Hang Ga Street, Hoan Kiem<br/><br/>Rice batter steamed into paper-thin sheets to order over a cloth-covered pot, rolled around minced pork and wood-ear mushroom, eaten with fish sauce and fried shallot.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> A breakfast dish you have to watch being made, and the Old Quarter's Bib Gourmand address for it<br/><a href="https://guide.michelin.com/en/vn/ha-noi/ha-noi_2974158/restaurants/bib-gourmand">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://guide.michelin.com/en/ha-noi/ha-noi_2974158/restaurant/banh-cuon-gia-truyen-thanh-van-hang-ga-street">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0360528,105.8471707&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0360528, 105.8471707]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8471707,21.0360528,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Cafe Giang</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-street_food</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Old Quarter, 39 Nguyen Huu Huan, down an alley<br/><br/>The family cafe of Nguyen Van Giang, the Metropole hotel bartender who invented egg coffee in the 1940s by whipping egg yolk with sugar in place of scarce milk. Run by his descendants since 1946.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The original version of the drink every Hanoi list says not to miss. The entrance is a narrow alley that is easy to walk past<br/><a href="https://cafegiang.vn/about-us/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egg_coffee">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0342713,105.8544991&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0342713, 105.8544991]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8544991,21.0342713,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Bun Thang Ba Duc</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-street_food</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Old Quarter, 48 Cau Go<br/><br/>An evening-only shop that has served bun thang for over thirty years - a clear chicken broth over thin rice noodles topped with shredded chicken, strips of egg omelette and pork sausage, a dish built from many small preparations.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Bun thang is a Hanoi dish rarely found elsewhere, and this is one of the long-running addresses for it, 300m from the lake. Open roughly 16:00 to 23:00<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Restaurant_Review-g293924-d8845163-Reviews-Bun_Thang_Ba_Duc-Hanoi.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://antoanthucpham.hanoi.gov.vn/bun-thang-ba-duc-giu-tron-net-tinh-te-cua-mon-an-cau-ky-bac-nhat-ha-thanh-239260410103331394.htm">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.032057,105.8521518&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.032057, 105.8521518]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8521518,21.032057,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Pho Cuon Hung Ben</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-street_food</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Truc Bach, 118 Tran Vu<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>Fresh sheets of uncut pho noodle rolled around stir-fried beef and herbs and dipped in sweet fish sauce. The dish was invented on the Ngu Xa peninsula of Truc Bach Lake, and Hung Ben is one of its oldest shops, since moved from 33 Ngu Xa to a larger room on Tran Vu.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Pho cuon is a Hanoi-only dish, and the shop sits by Truc Bach Lake, two minutes from the shrimp-cake stall on Thanh Nien<br/><a href="https://www.foody.vn/ha-noi/pho-cuon-hung-ben-118-tran-vu">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://riviu.vn/pho-cuon-hung-ben">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0459439,105.8408929&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0459439, 105.8408929]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8408929,21.0459439,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Banh Tom Ho Tay</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-street_food</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Truc Bach, 1 Thanh Nien, between West Lake and Truc Bach Lake<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A lakeside restaurant known for one dish, crisp fritters of sweet potato topped with whole shrimp, eaten wrapped in lettuce and herbs with a sour dipping sauce.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The address CNN called the one food to try in Hanoi, on the road between the two lakes where it is best eaten at sunset. Expect tourist prices and simple service<br/><a href="https://www.cnn.com/travel/article/shrimp-cakes-hanoi-vietnam">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Restaurant_Review-g293924-d2687040-Reviews-Banh_tom_Ho_Tay-Hanoi.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0473212,105.8376261&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0473212, 105.8376261]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8376261,21.0473212,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Cha Ca Thang Long</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-restaurants</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Old Quarter, Duong Thanh street<br/><br/>A no-frills shop where turmeric-marinated fish is grilled tableside on a charcoal brazier, then finished in a pan with dill and spring onion and served over rice noodles.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Michelin Bib Gourmand and the most consistently recommended cha ca address left in the Old Quarter<br/><a href="https://guide.michelin.com/us/en/ha-noi/ha-noi_2974158/restaurant/cha-ca-thang-long">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.033293,105.8459895&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.033293, 105.8459895]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8459895,21.033293,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Gia</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-restaurants</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Tay Ho (West Lake)<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A small tasting-menu restaurant built around a chef's-table kitchen, serving a set multi-course menu that reworks Vietnamese ingredients in a modern fine-dining format.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> One of Vietnam's first Michelin-starred restaurants (awarded 2025, retained 2026), and Hanoi's clearest high-end tasting-menu option<br/><a href="https://guide.michelin.com/us/en/article/michelin-guide-ceremony/michelin-guide-vietnam-2026">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0205027,105.7639271&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0205027, 105.7639271]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.7639271,21.0205027,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Luk Lak</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-restaurants</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Hai Ba Trung<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A sit-down Vietnamese restaurant in a colonial-era villa, run by a chef who cooked for the diplomatic corps, serving regional dishes as a full menu rather than a single speciality.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The one address here that works as a proper dinner with a table and a wine list while still being Bib Gourmand priced<br/><a href="https://guide.michelin.com/en/vn/ha-noi/ha-noi_2974158/restaurants/bib-gourmand">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://en.vietnamplus.vn/michelin-guide-unveils-2024-bib-gourmand-selection-for-hanoi-hcm-city-post289000.vnp">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0225054,105.8575276&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0225054, 105.8575276]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8575276,21.0225054,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Uu Dam Chay</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-restaurants</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Hoan Kiem<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A multi-floor vegetarian kitchen that rebuilds Vietnamese classics without meat or fish sauce, presented as a full plated menu.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The only Bib Gourmand vegetarian address in Hanoi, and the fallback when a meat-heavy week has run its course<br/><a href="https://guide.michelin.com/en/vn/ha-noi/ha-noi_2974158/restaurants/bib-gourmand">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://happyvietnamtours.com/blogs/news/michelin-guide-vietnam-2024-michelin-star-bib-gourmand">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0187719,105.84814&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0187719, 105.84814]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.84814,21.0187719,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Thang Co A Quynh</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-sapa-restaurants</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Sapa</b> &middot; 15 Thach Son Street, Sapa town<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A long-running local restaurant serving thang co, the H'mong horse-meat and offal stew simmered with highland herbs, alongside other mountain specialities.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The most consistently recommended address in Sapa for this specific regional dish, with an established following on Tripadvisor and local guides alike<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Restaurant_Review-g311304-d12951605-Reviews-Thang_Co_A_Quynh_Restaurant-Sapa_Lao_Cai_Province.html">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=22.3360931,103.8445752&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 22.3360931, 103.8445752]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>103.8445752,22.3360931,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Duc De Restaurant</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-ninh-binh-restaurants</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ninh Binh</b> &middot; City center, Ninh Binh<br/><br/>A long-running goat-meat restaurant on a main street in Ninh Binh city, serving the mountain goat (de nui) dishes the region is known for - steamed, grilled and in hotpot.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Consistently the most-reviewed goat-meat specialist in the city center, with a reputation built over years rather than a recent tourist spike<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Restaurant_Review-g303945-d8853028-Reviews-Duc_De_Restaurant-Ninh_Binh_Ninh_Binh_Province.html">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=20.2340333,105.9682771&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 20.2340333, 105.9682771]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.9682771,20.2340333,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Trung Tuyet</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-ninh-binh-street_food</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ninh Binh</b> &middot; City center, Ninh Binh<br/><br/>A local eatery specializing in com chay - sun-dried rice pressed into crisp sheets, deep-fried and served with a savory meat-and-mushroom sauce - alongside egg soup and pho.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Ranked among the most reliable all-rounders in Ninh Binh on both Tripadvisor and Lonely Planet for the region's signature souvenir dish<br/><a href="https://pioneersailtravel.com/ninh-binh-restaurants/">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=20.2516139,105.97874&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 20.2516139, 105.97874]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.97874,20.2516139,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Bun Bo Hue Ba Gai</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hue-street_food</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hue</b> &middot; 11A Ha Noi Street, Vinh Ninh<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A 24-hour noodle shop serving bun bo Hue, the city's own spicy lemongrass-and-beef broth noodle soup, in a plain no-frills dining room popular with locals at all hours.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> One of the most consistently recommended bun bo Hue spots in the city that actually invented the dish, open around the clock<br/><a href="https://www.willflyforfood.net/hue-food-guide/">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=16.4639321,107.5863388&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 16.4639321, 107.5863388]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>107.5863388,16.4639321,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Lac Thien (Banh Khoai Lac Thien)</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hue-restaurants</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hue</b> &middot; 6 Dinh Tien Hoang Street<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A family-run restaurant grilling banh khoai to order - Hue's crisp turmeric rice-flour pancake, folded over pork, shrimp and bean sprouts and eaten wrapped in rice paper with a thick peanut-sesame dipping sauce.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Widely cited as Hue's best banh khoai, and the crunchiest version of the dish in the city<br/><a href="https://beebeetravel.com/banh-khoai-lac-thien-hue-local-food/">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=16.4720712,107.5846611&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 16.4720712, 107.5846611]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>107.5846611,16.4720712,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Banh Mi Phuong</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-street_food</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; 2B Phan Chu Trinh Street, Old Town<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A perpetually busy banh mi stand grilling and assembling its own pork, pate and pickled vegetables into a crusty baguette sandwich to order.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The banh mi Anthony Bourdain called possibly the best in Vietnam - still packed with a line out the door years later<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Restaurant_Review-g298082-d2365673-Reviews-Banh_My_Phu_ng-Hoi_An_Quang_Nam_Province.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://hiddenhoian.com/eat/banh-mi-phuong-hoi-ans-best-banh-mi/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=15.8784841,108.3320081&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 15.8784841, 108.3320081]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.3320081,15.8784841,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Phu Hong (Bun Thit Nuong Phu Hong)</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-street_food</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; 19 Yen Bai Street, Hai Chau District, Da Nang<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A street-food stall serving bun thit nuong - grilled pork over rice vermicelli with fresh herbs - alongside grilled pork and minced-pork skewers wrapped in rice paper, no reservations, cash only.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Holds a Michelin Bib Gourmand for good quality, good value cooking - the everyday-food end of Da Nang's Michelin coverage, distinct from La Maison 1888's fine dining<br/><a href="https://liveyounglivewell.com/quan-phu-hong-da-nang-review/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://guide.michelin.com/en/da-nang-region/da-nang_2984390/restaurant/phu-hong">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=16.0695853,108.2229138&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 16.0695853, 108.2229138]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.2229138,16.0695853,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>My Quang Sua Hong Van</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-street_food</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; Da Nang<br/><br/>A stall serving my quang - central Vietnam's wide turmeric-yellow noodles in a shallow pool of broth rather than a soup - in the local jellyfish version, with peanuts and a sheet of grilled rice cracker.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> My quang is the dish of this province specifically, and the jellyfish version barely exists outside Da Nang<br/><a href="https://guide.michelin.com/us/en/da-nang-region/restaurants/bib-gourmand">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Restaurants-g298085-Da_Nang.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=16.0661233,108.229693&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 16.0661233, 108.229693]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.229693,16.0661233,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Banh Beo - Banh Dap</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-street_food</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; Da Nang<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A stall built on two central-coast snacks - small steamed rice cakes topped with dried shrimp, and a grilled rice cracker sandwiched with a soft wet sheet that you smash by hand before dipping.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Banh dap is eaten by breaking it with your palm, which makes it the most fun dish on this list and one you will not find at home<br/><a href="https://guide.michelin.com/us/en/da-nang-region/restaurants/bib-gourmand">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Restaurants-g298085-Da_Nang.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=15.8844674,108.3303903&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 15.8844674, 108.3303903]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.3303903,15.8844674,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Co Chu Nho</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-street_food</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; Da Nang<br/><br/>A small Da Nang street-food shop that entered the Bib Gourmand list in the most recent selection.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> A Bib Gourmand that sits away from the riverfront circuit, so it has not been reshaped by tour-group traffic the way the central addresses have<br/><a href="https://guide.michelin.com/vn/en/da-nang-region/da-nang_2984390/restaurant/co-chu-nho">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://guide.michelin.com/us/en/da-nang-region/restaurants/bib-gourmand">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=16.0461704,108.2454747&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 16.0461704, 108.2454747]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.2454747,16.0461704,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>White Rose Restaurant</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-restaurants</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; 533 Hai Ba Trung Street<br/><br/>The restaurant most associated with white rose dumplings (banh bao vac) - translucent rice-dough parcels of spiced minced shrimp, steamed and shaped to resemble a folded rose, then topped with crispy fried shallots.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The dish comes from one family recipe distributed to eateries around town, and this restaurant is the most famous outlet for it<br/><a href="https://culturephamtravel.com/white-rose-dumplings-hoi-an/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://cavinteo.blogspot.com/2024/10/white-rose-restaurant-dumplings-hoi-an-vietnam.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=15.8829593,108.3250054&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 15.8829593, 108.3250054]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.3250054,15.8829593,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>La Maison 1888</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-restaurants</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; InterContinental Danang Sun Peninsula Resort, Son Tra<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A fine-dining French restaurant inside a recreated Indochinese colonial mansion overlooking the sea, serving a five- or eight-course set menu built on Vietnamese, French and Japanese ingredients.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The only Michelin-starred restaurant in Central Vietnam, held by chef Christian Le Squer, who carried three Michelin stars in France for 23 years<br/><a href="https://guide.michelin.com/us/en/da-nang-region/da-nang_2984390/restaurant/la-maison-1888">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.danang.intercontinental.com/a-michelin-star-for-la-maison-1888/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=15.9040037,108.3326237&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 15.9040037, 108.3326237]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.3326237,15.9040037,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Thia Go</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-restaurants</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; Da Nang<br/><br/>A sit-down Vietnamese kitchen working with central-coast produce and seafood, plated as a full menu rather than a single-dish counter.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Bib Gourmand, and the option for a proper dinner in Da Nang that is neither a resort restaurant nor a plastic stool<br/><a href="https://guide.michelin.com/us/en/da-nang-region/da-nang_2984390/restaurant/thia-go%C2%A0">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://guide.michelin.com/us/en/da-nang-region/restaurants/bib-gourmand">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=16.0532123,108.24153&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 16.0532123, 108.24153]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.24153,16.0532123,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Be Ni 2</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-restaurants</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; Da Nang<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A Da Nang seafood house where the catch is picked from tanks at the front and cooked to order - grilled, steamed with lemongrass, or fried with tamarind.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The only seafood address on the guide's Da Nang list, in a city whose whole economy faces the sea<br/><a href="https://guide.michelin.com/us/en/da-nang-region/restaurants/bib-gourmand">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Restaurants-g298085-Da_Nang.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=16.0667909,108.2449525&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 16.0667909, 108.2449525]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.2449525,16.0667909,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Ngoc Chi</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-restaurants</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; Da Nang<br/><br/>A vegetarian kitchen rebuilding central-Vietnamese dishes without meat or fish sauce, from noodle bowls to rice plates.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Bib Gourmand vegetarian, and the fallback in a region whose cooking leans hard on fish sauce and pork<br/><a href="https://guide.michelin.com/us/en/da-nang-region/restaurants/bib-gourmand">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Restaurants-g298085-Da_Nang.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=16.0650511,108.2227097&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 16.0650511, 108.2227097]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.2227097,16.0650511,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Banh Mi Huynh Hoa</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-saigon-street_food</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ho Chi Minh City</b> &middot; District 1<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A takeaway counter open since 1989 with a permanent line out front, packing a baguette with five cold cuts, pate, butter and pickles.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Widely cited as the single most-loaded, most-recommended banh mi in the city, voted Travelers' Top Pick at the 2024 Flavors Awards<br/><a href="https://vietcetera.com/en/banh-mi-huynh-hoa-how-100k-and-35-years-built-a-saigon-icon">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Restaurant_Review-g293925-d5484355-Reviews-Banh_Mi_Huynh_Hoa-Ho_Chi_Minh_City.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.7713922,106.6927189&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.7713922, 106.6927189]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.6927189,10.7713922,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Pho Phuong</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-saigon-street_food</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ho Chi Minh City</b> &middot; District 3<br/><br/>A southern pho counter, sweeter and more herb-heavy than the Hanoi version, served with the plate of bean sprouts, basil and lime that the north leaves off.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Eating this after Hanoi's pho is the fastest way to taste how far apart the two halves of the country actually are<br/><a href="https://guide.michelin.com/us/en/ho-chi-minh/restaurants/bib-gourmand">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Restaurants-g293925-Ho_Chi_Minh_City.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.7747697,106.7007331&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.7747697, 106.7007331]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.7007331,10.7747697,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Anan Saigon</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-saigon-restaurants</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ho Chi Minh City</b> &middot; District 1, Cho Cu (Old Market)<br/><br/>A restaurant built inside a former wet-market stall on Ton That Dam Street, where chef Peter Cuong Franklin runs street-food recipes through French fine-dining technique.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> One Michelin Star, held since 2023, and the clearest example of Saigon's fine-dining scene built on its own street food<br/><a href="https://guide.michelin.com/en/ho-chi-minh/ho-chi-minh_2978179/restaurant/anan-saigon">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://anansaigon.com/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.7718032,106.7029748&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.7718032, 106.7029748]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.7029748,10.7718032,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Cuc Gach Quan</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-saigon-restaurants</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ho Chi Minh City</b> &middot; District 1<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>An architect-converted old house serving the kind of everyday Vietnamese home cooking - braises, clay pots, simple greens - that restaurants usually skip, across several small rooms and a courtyard.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Bib Gourmand, and the answer to a week of noodle counters when what you want is a table, a fan and a long dinner<br/><a href="https://guide.michelin.com/us/en/ho-chi-minh/restaurants/bib-gourmand">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Restaurants-g293925-Ho_Chi_Minh_City.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.7929997,106.6889296&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.7929997, 106.6889296]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.6889296,10.7929997,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Hum Garden</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-saigon-restaurants</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ho Chi Minh City</b> &middot; District 1<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A garden restaurant serving plated vegetarian Vietnamese food - lotus, tofu, mushroom and herb dishes - in a quiet courtyard away from the traffic.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Saigon's Bib Gourmand vegetarian address, and one of the few genuinely calm dining rooms in District 1<br/><a href="https://guide.michelin.com/us/en/ho-chi-minh/restaurants/bib-gourmand">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Restaurants-g293925-Ho_Chi_Minh_City.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.804141,106.7441455&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.804141, 106.7441455]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.7441455,10.804141,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Banh Xeo 46A</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-saigon-restaurants</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ho Chi Minh City</b> &middot; District 1<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A long-running shop turning out large turmeric-yellow rice crepes filled with pork, prawn and bean sprouts, wrapped at the table in mustard leaf and herbs.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Bib Gourmand, and the southern dish that is most fun to eat with your hands<br/><a href="https://guide.michelin.com/us/en/ho-chi-minh/restaurants/bib-gourmand">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Restaurants-g293925-Ho_Chi_Minh_City.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.7896047,106.6912995&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.7896047, 106.6912995]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.6912995,10.7896047,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Lua Nep</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-mekong-restaurants</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Mekong and Can Tho</b> &middot; Ninh Kieu riverside, Can Tho<br/><br/>A riverside restaurant serving the delta's signature elephant ear fish, fried whole and served upright so the flesh is rolled into rice paper with herbs and pickles at the table.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Repeatedly singled out by recent travelers for this specific dish, the Mekong Delta's most iconic<br/><a href="https://hanoivoyage.com/en/blog/restaurant/top-7-restaurants-in-the-mekong-delta">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.0450175,105.7947956&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.0450175, 105.7947956]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.7947956,10.0450175,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sao Hom Restaurant</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-mekong-restaurants</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Mekong and Can Tho</b> &middot; Ninh Kieu riverside, Can Tho, inside the old Nha Long market building<br/><br/>A restaurant set inside a restored early-1900s riverside market hall, with open river views and a menu of southern Vietnamese home cooking.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> A local guide recommendation for a proper sit-down dinner, in one of Can Tho's most distinctive dining buildings<br/><a href="https://vinwonders.com/en/wonderpedia/news/can-tho-restaurants/">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.0313789,105.7882653&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.0313789, 105.7882653]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.7882653,10.0313789,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
    </Folder>
    <Folder>
      <name>Markets &amp; Shopping</name>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Dong Xuan Market</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-markets</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Old Quarter, 600m north of Hoan Kiem Lake<br/><br/>Hanoi's largest covered market, three floors and five linked pavilion halls over 6,500 square metres, selling everything from textiles and household goods to a ground-floor food and produce section.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The working market Hanoians actually shop at, not a tourist-only stall row<br/><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C4%90%E1%BB%93ng_Xu%C3%A2n_Market">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0382556,105.849671&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0382556, 105.849671]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.849671,21.0382556,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Hanoi Weekend Night Market</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-markets</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Old Quarter, Hang Dao / Hang Ngang / Hang Luoc streets<br/><br/>A near-3km stretch of Old Quarter streets closed to traffic on Friday, Saturday and Sunday evenings and filled with clothing and souvenir stalls, street food carts, and impromptu street performances.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Only runs three nights a week, so it is worth timing the Hanoi stay around if the dates line up<br/><a href="https://vinpearl.com/en/hanoi-weekend-night-market">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0335078,105.8509571&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0335078, 105.8509571]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8509571,21.0335078,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Hang Gai Silk Street</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-markets</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Old Quarter, Hang Gai street<br/><br/>A 300m street of more than a hundred shops selling silk scarves, embroidered clothing, tailoring and lacquerware, on the old route from the river to the citadel's south gate.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The place to buy silk and have something tailored, a few minutes from Hoan Kiem Lake. Quality varies widely from shop to shop, so compare before paying<br/><a href="https://vinpearl.com/en/hanoi-silk-street">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0323251,105.851018&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0323251, 105.851018]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.851018,21.0323251,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Cho Rong (Rong Market)</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-ninh-binh-markets</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ninh Binh</b> &middot; 71 Van Giang Street, Thanh Binh Ward<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>Ninh Binh city's main traditional market, spread across a three-story building and open-air stalls, selling household goods, textiles and a fresh-produce section with rice, fish, mountain goat meat and Kim Son rice wine.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The everyday market locals actually shop at, as opposed to the tourist-facing night market<br/><a href="https://hanoiexploretravel.com/things-to-do-ninh-binh/rong-market">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=20.2561711,105.9776166&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 20.2561711, 105.9776166]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.9776166,20.2561711,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Dong Ba Market</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hue-markets</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hue</b> &middot; North bank of the Perfume River, city center<br/><br/>Hue's biggest market, a three-floor bell-shaped building where the ground floor sells dried seafood and local sauces, the second floor sells crafts like forged knives and ceramics, and the third is devoted to ao dai tailoring.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Central Vietnam's largest and oldest market, and as much a symbol of Hue's identity as the Citadel<br/><a href="https://vinwonders.com/en/wonderpedia/news/dong-ba-market-take-a-tour-to-one-of-the-busiest-markets-in-hue/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://vietnamdiscovery.com/hue/shopping/dong-ba-market/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=16.4724474,107.5886452&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 16.4724474, 107.5886452]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>107.5886452,16.4724474,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Buon Ma Thuot Central Market</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-buon-ma-thuot-markets</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Buon Ma Thuot</b> &middot; City center, near Victory Monument<br/><br/>The city's main covered market, stalls piled with roasted and green coffee beans, black pepper, cacao, macadamia nuts and honey harvested from the surrounding highland forests.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The place to buy coffee straight from the source rather than at inflated airport prices<br/><a href="https://www.frommers.com/destinations/buon-ma-thuot/shopping/overview">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://vinpearl.com/en/buon-ma-thuot-vietnam-a-comprehensive-travel-guide">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=12.6805026,108.042465&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 12.6805026, 108.042465]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.042465,12.6805026,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Da Lat Market (Cho Da Lat)</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-da-lat-markets</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Da Lat</b> &middot; City center, beside Xuan Huong Lake<br/><br/>The city's main covered market building on a hillside above Xuan Huong Lake, selling fresh produce, dried flowers, hot street food and Da Lat's own coffee and wine.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The daytime anchor of Da Lat's market district, a few steps from the lake<br/><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Da_Lat_Market">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://vinwonders.com/en/wonderpedia/news/dalat-market/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=11.9435196,108.4372097&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 11.9435196, 108.4372097]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.4372097,11.9435196,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Da Lat Night Market (Cho Am Phu)</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-da-lat-markets</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Da Lat</b> &middot; Steps below Da Lat Market, city center<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>The streets and steps below the market building come alive after 5pm with food stalls, grilled rice paper, hot soy milk and fresh strawberries, plus clothing and souvenir stalls.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Da Lat's evening centre of gravity, and where most of the specialty street food listed here is actually eaten<br/><a href="https://vinpearl.com/en/da-lat-night-market">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.vietnamtourism.org.vn/travel-guide/destination-in-vietnam/da-lat%E2%80%99s-night-market.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=11.9414964,108.4372724&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 11.9414964, 108.4372724]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.4372724,11.9414964,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Ben Thanh Market</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-saigon-markets</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ho Chi Minh City</b> &middot; District 1<br/><br/>A covered market of nearly 1,450 stalls under a French-built 1914 clock-tower facade, selling everything from produce and dried goods to clothing, souvenirs and a food court, spilling into a street-food night market outside after dark.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The city's oldest and largest market, and the default orientation point for District 1<br/><a href="https://oxalisadventure.com/ben-thanh-market-ho-chi-minh-city/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://vinwonders.com/en/wonderpedia/news/ben-thanh-market-a-prominent-landmark-of-the-bustling-ho-chi-minh-city/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.7725301,106.6980365&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.7725301, 106.6980365]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.6980365,10.7725301,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Phu Quoc Night Market (Dinh Cau)</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-phu-quoc-markets</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Phu Quoc</b> &middot; Duong Dong, Bach Dang street<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A single-street night market where diners pick live fish, crab or squid from tanks to be grilled or steamed to order, alongside stalls selling herring salad, banh xeo pancakes and grilled scallops.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The island's main after-dark food destination since its 2016 merger with the old Dinh Cau market, and the best single stop for trying local seafood specialties in one place<br/><a href="https://www.baconismagic.ca/vietnam/phu-quoc-night-market/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://vinwonders.com/en/wonderpedia/news/phu-quoc-night-market/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.3242259,103.8581673&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.3242259, 103.8581673]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>103.8581673,10.3242259,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
    </Folder>
    <Folder>
      <name>Nightlife</name>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Ta Hien Beer Street (Bia Hoi Junction)</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-nightlife</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Ta Hien and Luong Ngoc Quyen, Old Quarter<br/><br/>A pedestrianised corner where plastic stools spill across the road each evening and fresh unpasteurised draught beer is poured from the keg for about 15,000 VND a glass.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Not a bar but the city's open-air living room, and the cheapest beer you will ever drink sitting down<br/><a href="https://www.hanoitourism.org/hanoi-bia-hoi-guide/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Attractions-g293924-Activities-c20-t99-Hanoi.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0341902,105.852079&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0341902, 105.852079]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.852079,21.0341902,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>An Bang Beach bars</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-nightlife</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; An Bang Beach, 3 km from the Old Town<br/><br/>A line of beachfront bars and seafood shacks with live music, sand underfoot and a very different crowd from the lantern-lit old town.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Where Hoi An goes when the old town closes at 22:00, and the only late option in this station<br/><a href="https://vietadvisor.travel/hoi-an/hoi-an-nightlife">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://hoianmemoriesresort.com/en/best-bars-in-hoi-an-top-nightlife-spots-for-tourists-and-young-travelers.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=15.9141779,108.3396551&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 15.9141779, 108.3396551]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.3396551,15.9141779,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Maze Bar (100 Roofs Cafe)</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-da-lat-nightlife</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Da Lat</b> &middot; Da Lat city centre, among the shop-houses<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A bar built as a labyrinth: five floors of small rooms, dark passages and spiral staircases wound through a shop-house, serving locally brewed beer. A cafe by day.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The most distinctive room in Da Lat with nothing like it elsewhere on the route - the building itself is the attraction, and the acoustic sets run Thursday to Saturday<br/><a href="https://theculturetrip.com/asia/vietnam/articles/the-8-best-bars-in-dalat-vietnam">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://dalat.app/blog/guides/da-lat-nightlife-bars-live-music-guide">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=11.9446106,108.4375604&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 11.9446106, 108.4375604]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.4375604,11.9446106,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Layla</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-saigon-nightlife</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ho Chi Minh City</b> &middot; Dong Du Street, District 1<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A bar spread across the upper floors of a colonial-era apartment block, with an eleven-metre bar in the main room and neighbouring flats converted into a pool room, lounges and a smoking terrace.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The clearest example of Saigon's converted-apartment-block bar culture, which exists nowhere else in the country<br/><a href="https://www.asia-bars.com/2026/03/top-12-highly-rated-cocktail-bars-hcmc-vietnam-2026/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Attractions-g293925-Activities-c20-t99-Ho_Chi_Minh_City.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.775022,106.7031275&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.775022, 106.7031275]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.7031275,10.775022,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sunset Town night street</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-phu-quoc-nightlife</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Phu Quoc</b> &middot; An Thoi, southern tip of the island<br/><br/>A purpose-built Mediterranean-style seafront quarter: a food street, beachfront bars, the Kiss Bridge, large evening shows and a nightly five-minute firework display.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The only organised night out on the island, and also where the An Thoi dive boats leave from - so the dive day and the evening are the same trip south<br/><a href="https://visitphuquoc.com.vn/en/explore-the-nightlife-at-sunset-town-9843">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://localvietnam.com/phu-quoc/nightlife-in-phu-quoc/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.0294252,104.0083142&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.0294252, 104.0083142]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>104.0083142,10.0294252,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
    </Folder>
    <Folder>
      <name>Spa &amp; Massage</name>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Orient Spa Hanoi</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-spa</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Old Quarter, Hoan Kiem (four branches)<br/><br/>A spa group running four branches inside the Old Quarter, offering foot and body massage, deep tissue work, and packages that combine massage with a hair wash.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The most consistently reviewed chain in the Old Quarter, and having four branches means one is always within walking distance of wherever the day ended<br/><a href="https://www.orientspahanoi.com/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com.sg/Attraction_Review-g293924-d24090474-Reviews-Hanoi_Old_Quarter_Spa-Hanoi.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.0289459,105.8487425&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.0289459, 105.8487425]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8487425,21.0289459,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Herbal Spa Hoi An</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-spa</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; Hoi An, several branches<br/><br/>A spa built around a Vietnamese herbal treatment - lemongrass, ginger, cinnamon and mugwort steamed and applied warm - alongside standard body and foot massage.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> 4.8 out of 5 from more than two thousand Klook reviews and top-ranked on TripAdvisor since 2019, which is a far deeper review base than any spa in Hanoi<br/><a href="https://herbalspa.vn/en/hoi-an">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Attractions-g298082-Activities-c40-Hoi_An_Quang_Nam_Province.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=15.8784079,108.3240194&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 15.8784079, 108.3240194]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.3240194,15.8784079,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Ocean Spa Hoi An</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-spa</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; Near An Bang Beach<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A spa near An Bang beach offering body, foot and herbal treatments away from the old-town crowds.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> 4.9 out of 5 across more than 1,300 reviews, and close enough to the beach to combine with a swim<br/><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Attractions-g298082-Activities-c40-Hoi_An_Quang_Nam_Province.html">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.klook.com/destination/c75-hoi-an/1011-spa/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=15.9129019,108.3402428&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 15.9129019, 108.3402428]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.3402428,15.9129019,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Phu Quoc Day Spa</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-phu-quoc-spa</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Phu Quoc</b> &middot; Duong Dong<br/><br/>The island's most-reviewed salon. Every session opens with a herbal foot soak and closes with tea and fruit; the signature is a two-hour hot stone and full-body treatment.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The island is the last stop before the flight home, and this is where the sunburn and the boat days get undone - book away from 19:00-21:00, when it backs up<br/><a href="https://vietnamspot.ru/en/blog/phu-quoc-spa">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.2001711,103.9646577&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.2001711, 103.9646577]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>103.9646577,10.2001711,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
    </Folder>
    <Folder>
      <name>Diving</name>
      <Placemark>
        <name>John's Tours - the quote on file</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-phu-quoc-diving</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Phu Quoc</b> &middot; Southern Phu Quoc, An Thoi; hotel pickup included<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>A direct dive club, not booked through an agency, which sent a detailed written quote - 110 USD per person for two dives of about 45-50 minutes, covering gear, a dive master, hotel pickup and return, water, fruit and lunch on the boat. Soft drinks and beer are extra and served after the dives. Payment is to the guide on the boat in cash, by Vietnamese bank transfer, or by card through OnePay with a 3 percent fee. For an uncertified diver it runs Discover Scuba one-to-one, 6 metres maximum, about 25 minutes a dive, age 10 and up, barred for heart, lung or blood-pressure conditions and pregnancy.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The only quoted price in this tab, and it sits inside the normal Phu Quoc range of 90 to 130 USD for two fun dives. Two things are still open - the club has not given a phone number or website, and the quote prices a certified diver, not the Discover Scuba slot, so that figure needs asking for. Note also that two agencies already offered An Thoi diving inside their packages, so check you are not buying the same day twice<br/><a href="https://localvietnam.com/phu-quoc/diving-in-phu-quoc/">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.1942845,103.9674837&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.1942845, 103.9674837]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>103.9674837,10.1942845,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Hon Thom (Pineapple Island) dive sites</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-phu-quoc-diving</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Phu Quoc</b> &middot; An Thoi archipelago, below the cable car<br/><br/>Two separate sites on the same island. North Pineapple Point reaches about 18 metres over a rocky bottom carrying a large colony of whip corals, with cuttlefish and reef fish. South Pineapple Point drops to 35 metres and is the deepest dive in Phu Quoc.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The north site is the better-value dive here for a PADI Open Water diver - its 18-metre bottom is exactly the Open Water limit, so the whole site is in range. The south site is not - 35 metres needs Advanced, and no amount of good visibility changes it<br/><a href="https://www.flipperdiving.com/phu-quoc-dive-spots/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://phuquocdivezone.com/dive-sites-phu-quoc/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=9.9920077,104.0124425&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 9.9920077, 104.0124425]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>104.0124425,9.9920077,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Hon Dua (Coco Island) and the porcelain wreck</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-phu-quoc-diving</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Phu Quoc</b> &middot; An Thoi archipelago, the island closest to An Thoi town<br/><i>Approximate location — search the name in Maps</i><br/><br/>The nearest island to An Thoi town, which makes it the shortest boat ride in the archipelago. Divers off its western shore report pieces of antique porcelain scattered around a sunken ship.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The one site here with something other than reef to look at, and the closest to port - which matters on a day the sea turns and the boat needs a short run home<br/><a href="https://saigontourism.com.vn/a-guide-to-diving-the-phu-quoc-islands/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://phuquocdivezone.com/dive-sites-phu-quoc/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=9.996007,104.0104165&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 9.996007, 104.0104165]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>104.0104165,9.996007,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
    </Folder>
    <Folder>
      <name>Logistics</name>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Noi Bai Airport to Old Quarter transfer</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hanoi-logistics</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hanoi</b> &middot; Noi Bai International Airport to Hoan Kiem<br/><br/>The airport sits about 27-30km north of the Old Quarter; a metered taxi or pre-booked private transfer takes 30-40 minutes off-peak (up to 90 in traffic) and costs roughly 300,000-350,000 VND, while a Grab typically comes in cheaper at around 240,000-250,000 VND plus tolls.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The first and last transfer of the whole trip, and the one most worth pre-booking to skip the arrivals-hall taxi touts<br/><a href="https://eternalarrival.com/grab-at-hanoi-airport-noi-bai/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://unchartedlens.com/routes/vietnam/hanoi-airport-to-city-transport-comparison/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=21.2188925,105.8044596&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 21.2188925, 105.8044596]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>105.8044596,21.2188925,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Phu Bai Airport to Hue city center</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hue-logistics</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hue</b> &middot; Airport transfer<br/><br/>Phu Bai International Airport sits about 15km south of Hue; a taxi or private car to the city center takes 20-30 minutes and costs roughly 250,000-300,000 VND ($10-12).<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Fixes the transfer time for flying in or out of Hue directly, rather than via Da Nang<br/><a href="https://www.geckoroutes.com/vietnam/hue-airport/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://danangtransfer.vn/en/4-ways-to-get-from-hue-airport-to-city-center-price-time-tips/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=16.399655,107.7054596&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 16.399655, 107.7054596]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>107.7054596,16.399655,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Da Nang Airport to Hoi An</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-logistics</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; Airport transfer<br/><br/>Da Nang International Airport (DAD) sits about 30km from Hoi An; a Grab car or taxi takes 30-40 minutes in normal traffic (up to an hour at peak times) and costs roughly 250,000-400,000 VND.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Fixes the practical transfer time for the flight in and out of the Hoi An/Da Nang leg of the trip<br/><a href="https://unmappedasia.com/da-nang-airport-to-hoi-an/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.welcomepickups.com/da-nang/airport-to-hoi-an/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=16.0425792,108.1971613&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 16.0425792, 108.1971613]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.1971613,16.0425792,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Hoi An Old Town vehicle restrictions</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-hoi-an-logistics</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Hoi An and Da Nang</b> &middot; Old Town core<br/><br/>Cars and, at scheduled hours, motorbikes are barred from the Old Town's core streets, which are walking- and cyclo-only; a paid Old Town ticket covers entry to several assembly halls, temples and heritage houses.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Affects how you actually move around and reach hotels/restaurants once inside the historic core<br/><a href="https://grooviet.com/destination/japanese-covered-bridge/">source 1</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=15.8779509,108.3239898&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 15.8779509, 108.3239898]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>108.3239898,15.8779509,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Grab from Tan Son Nhat Airport</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-saigon-logistics</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ho Chi Minh City</b> &middot; Tan Son Nhat International Airport (SGN)<br/><br/>Ride-hailing pickup for Grab cars is outside the arrivals hall, past the metered-taxi rank; a ride to District 1 takes 25-50 minutes depending on traffic and costs roughly 110,000-250,000 VND.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Cheaper and more predictable than the taxi rank, with a fixed upfront fare shown before booking<br/><a href="https://blog.gettransfer.com/from-ho-chi-minh-city-airport-to-the-city-center-taxi-grab-more/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.grab.com/global/airport-rides/tan-son-nhat-international-airport/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.8179793,106.6562645&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.8179793, 106.6562645]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.6562645,10.8179793,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Terminal T3 is a separate building, not a gate</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-saigon-logistics</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ho Chi Minh City</b> &middot; Tan Son Nhat International Airport (SGN)<br/><br/>T3 opened in April 2025 and handles domestic flights only. There is no internal connection to T1 and T2 - moving between them means leaving the airport and going 3.3 to 3.8 km on public roads, 15 to 20 minutes by shuttle bus and longer in traffic. Vietnam Airlines domestic departures use T3; international departures stay at T2.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The single easiest way to miss a flight here. A domestic leg connecting to or from an international one is not a walk between gates - budget an hour on the ground, not fifteen minutes, and confirm which terminal the ticket says<br/><a href="https://www.traveloka.com/en-au/explore/tips/ho-chi-minh-city-airport-tan-son-nhat-airport-terminal-3/542434">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://authentiktravel.com/tan-son-nhat-t3-terminal">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.8179793,106.6562645&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.8179793, 106.6562645]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.6562645,10.8179793,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>No airport metro link yet</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-saigon-logistics</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Ho Chi Minh City</b> &middot; Tan Son Nhat International Airport (SGN)<br/><br/>Saigon's first metro line (Line 1, Ben Thanh-Suoi Tien) opened in December 2024, but no metro or rail line reaches the airport; a dedicated airport link is not expected until around 2030. Two underground stations serving all three terminals are planned on the Tan Son Nhat-Phu Huu line, still unbuilt. Long Thanh, the new airport out in Dong Nai, does not open commercially until 1 December 2026.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> Rules out the metro as an airport option, which travelers researching the new 2024 line sometimes assume is already connected - and rules out Long Thanh for this trip<br/><a href="https://aifly.one/2026/03/ho-chi-minh-city-tan-son-nhat-airport-guide/">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://e.vnexpress.net/news/news/traffic/ho-chi-minh-city-unveils-underground-metro-stations-to-link-all-3-tan-son-nhat-airport-terminals-5102103.html">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.8179793,106.6562645&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.8179793, 106.6562645]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>106.6562645,10.8179793,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Grab and taxi from Phu Quoc International Airport</name>
        <styleUrl>#s-phu-quoc-logistics</styleUrl>
        <description><![CDATA[<b>Phu Quoc</b> &middot; Phu Quoc International Airport (PQC), about 10 km south of Duong Dong<br/><br/>Grab and metered taxis both operate from the terminal; a ride to the Long Beach hotel strip takes about 10-15 minutes and costs roughly 100,000-200,000 VND, with Grab typically 10-20% cheaper than the metered taxi rate.<br/><b>Why it's here:</b> The airport sits close enough to the main hotel strip that a transfer is short and cheap regardless of which resort is booked<br/><a href="https://www.airporttransferportal.com/airport-guides/pqc/cost-to-city">source 1</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.grab.com/global/airport-rides/phu-quoc-international-airport/">source 2</a><br/><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=10.1727084,103.9921557&travelmode=driving" style="color:#0f7c5c;font-weight:700">Directions</a><br/>Coords: 10.1727084, 103.9921557]]></description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>103.9921557,10.1727084,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
    </Folder>
  </Document>
</kml>
